PDSA.topics["greedy"]={"id":"greedy","name":"Greedy Algorithms","tagline":"Pick the best-looking choice now and never undo it. Learn when that is provably right.","accent":"#b04a8f","story":"Packing a suitcase, you grab the item you need most first. Greedy algorithms work like that: at every step take the choice that looks best right now, and never change your mind. It is fast and simple. The hard part is knowing when it is correct. For activity scheduling the rule \"earliest finish first\" provably works; for coin change with strange coins it fails. Huffman coding, which compresses text, is a greedy algorithm that always merges the two lightest symbols.","mindmap":{"center":"Greedy","branches":[{"t":"Huffman","kids":["merge two smallest","prefix codes","sum f x length"]},{"t":"Intervals","kids":["earliest finish","rooms = peak","sweep line"]},{"t":"Lateness","kids":["earliest deadline","running sum","exchange argument"]},{"t":"Counter-examples","kids":["coins 1,3,4","not always optimal","DP vs greedy"]},{"t":"Costs","kids":["sort $n\\log n$","heap $n\\log n$","scan $n$"]}]},"connect":[["Min-heap","Huffman","picks smallest"],["Sorting","Activity selection","by finish"],["Exchange argument","Correctness","proof idea"],["Greedy choice","Dijkstra and Prim","next topic"]],"roadmap":[["Position map","map"],["What greedy means, and when it works","idea"],["Huffman coding: shorter codes for common symbols","huff"],["Interval scheduling and meeting rooms","ivl"],["Minimizing the maximum lateness","late"],["One-page comparison","hub"]],"prereqs":[],"sections":[{"id":"map","title":"Where this topic sits and a self-check","studying":"The greedy method and how it leads into Dijkstra and Prim.","why":"Huffman uses a min-heap from the last topic, and Graphs uses the same \"take the cheapest next\" idea.","plain":"Make the best choice now, never undo it. Sometimes this is provably optimal.","blocks":[{"t":"fig","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 500 220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"order\" style=\"max-width:500px\"><defs><marker id=\"fa\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" fill=\"#667088\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"130.0\" y1=\"130.0\" x2=\"162.0\" y2=\"130.0\" stroke=\"#667088\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><text x=\"146.0\" y=\"125.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\" font-style=\"italic\">min-heap</text><line x1=\"305.0\" y1=\"130.0\" x2=\"337.0\" y2=\"130.0\" stroke=\"#667088\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" marker-end=\"url(#fa)\"/><text x=\"321.0\" y=\"125.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\" font-style=\"italic\">greedy choice</text><rect x=\"10\" y=\"100\" width=\"120\" height=\"60\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#667088 12%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#667088\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"127.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">3. Heaps</text><text x=\"70.0\" y=\"141.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"400\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">min-heap</text><rect x=\"165\" y=\"100\" width=\"140\" height=\"60\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#b04a8f 12%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#b04a8f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"235.0\" y=\"120.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">4. Greedy</text><text x=\"235.0\" y=\"134.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"400\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">YOU ARE HERE</text><text x=\"235.0\" y=\"148.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"400\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">Huffman, scheduling</text><rect x=\"340\" y=\"100\" width=\"130\" height=\"60\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#d0463b 12%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#d0463b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"405.0\" y=\"127.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">5. Graphs</text><text x=\"405.0\" y=\"141.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" font-weight=\"400\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">Dijkstra, Prim</text></svg>","caption":"Learning order. Purple is this topic."},{"t":"table","head":["You need from before","Used here for"],"rows":[["min-heap (Heaps)","Huffman merging in $O(n\\log n)$, meeting rooms"],["sorting and $O(n\\log n)$ (Divide and Conquer)","cost of the sort step"],["tree depth and leaves (Trees)","Huffman code length = leaf depth"]]},{"t":"co","kind":"tip","md":"Marks: 26 PYQs, 84 of 500 marks (17 percent): Huffman 11 (35 marks), interval scheduling 9 (31), lateness 3 (9), general greedy 3 (9). [ADDED] About 75 minutes for a first read.","title":"Marks"},{"t":"fig","bar":{"labels":["Huffman","Intervals","Lateness","General"],"values":[35,31,9,9],"title":"Marks per idea (total 84)","xlabel":"idea","hl":[0]},"caption":"Marks per idea."},{"t":"co","kind":"def","md":"Self-check: sort `[(3,5),(1,2),(4,9)]` by the second number; compute $50\\cdot1+20\\cdot2$; say what a binary tree leaf is.","title":"Self-check"}],"remember":["Greedy = best choice now, never reconsidered. Needs a proof or it may fail."],"quiz":[{"id":"gr-q13","concept":"Self-check","type":"SA","stem":"$50\\cdot1+20\\cdot2+2\\cdot3=$ ?","num":{"ans":96,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"50+40+6.","sec":"map"},{"id":"gr-q14","concept":"Self-check","type":"MCQ","stem":"Which structure gives \"the two smallest\" quickly?","options":["stack","queue","min-heap","array"],"key":[2],"diff":"easy","explain":"A min-heap pops the smallest in $O(\\log n)$.","why":{"A":"","B":"","C":"Correct.","D":""},"sec":"map"}]},{"id":"idea","title":"What greedy means, and when it works","studying":"The greedy method: make the best-looking choice now and never undo it.","why":"Four PYQs ask whether a greedy rule is correct (coins, doubling, statements), and every other greedy topic here depends on the same test.","plain":"Greedy = pick what looks best right now. It is fast, but only some problems let it be correct.","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"A **greedy algorithm** builds a solution step by step. At each step it picks the option that looks best **right now** (the largest coin, the earliest finishing meeting, the two smallest weights) and **never reconsiders**. It is usually simple and fast, often $O(n\\log n)$ because of a sort or a heap."},{"t":"co","kind":"def","md":"**Greedy choice + no regret.** A greedy algorithm repeatedly makes a locally best choice and commits to it. It is *correct* only when a proof shows that the locally best choice can always be extended to a globally best answer.","title":"Definition"},{"t":"co","kind":"warn","md":"Greedy does **not** always give the optimal answer. PYQ statement \"the greedy technique always gives an optimized solution\" is **false**. Also, Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall are **dynamic programming**, not greedy; Dijkstra and Prim are greedy.","title":"Two facts the exam tests"},{"t":"p","md":"**Analogy:** hiking up a mountain in fog by always stepping uphill. You may reach a small hill and stop. **Limit:** if the mountain has only one peak (a \"greedy-friendly\" shape), always going uphill works."},{"t":"p","md":"**The standard proof idea (exchange argument).** Take any optimal solution that does something different from greedy at the first step. Show you can swap its first choice for greedy's choice **without making the answer worse**. Repeat, and the optimal solution turns into the greedy one. We use this idea for scheduling and for Huffman."},{"t":"p","md":"**How to disprove a greedy rule.** Find one small input where greedy gives a worse answer than another method. One such input is a **counter-example** and kills the rule."},{"t":"table","head":["Problem","Greedy rule","Optimal?","Why / counter-example"],"rows":[["activity selection (most activities)","earliest **finish** time first","**yes**","exchange argument"],["activity selection","earliest start first","no","one long early activity blocks many short ones"],["activity selection","shortest first","no","a short activity can sit in the middle and block two others"],["min max lateness","earliest **deadline** first","**yes**","exchange argument"],["min max lateness","shortest job first","no","a short job with a far deadline delays an urgent one"],["Huffman coding","merge two smallest","**yes**","exchange argument"],["coin change (any coins)","largest coin first","no","coins 1, 7, 10... see below"],["coin change (1, 2, 5, 10, 25 style)","largest coin first","yes for these denominations","special structure"],["shortest path with negative edges","Dijkstra's greedy choice","no","a later negative edge can shorten a finished vertex"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Coin counter-example, computed.** Coins $\\{1,6,7,10,15\\}$, pay 35. Greedy takes 15, 15, then 5 ones: that is **7 coins**. The optimum is 15+10+10 = **3 coins**. So largest-first is not optimal here."},{"t":"trace","head":["coins","amount","greedy (largest first)","optimal (DP check)","counter-example?"],"rows":[["[1, 2, 8, 9, 5, 10]","13","3","2","**yes**"],["[10, 1, 2, 5]","25","3","3","no"],["[6, 1, 5, 7]","15","3","3","no"],["[1, 6, 7, 10, 15]","35","7","3","**yes**"],["[1, 2, 5, 10, 25]","40","3","3","no"]],"title":"The five PYQ coin sets, checked by program","stepper":false,"noteLast":false,"foot":"The first and fourth sets are counter-examples."},{"t":"ex","level":"simple","title":"[ADDED] Disprove a greedy rule","problem":"Coins $\\{1,3,4\\}$, pay 6. Does largest-first work?","asked":"Yes or no.","idea":"Compare greedy with another split.","steps":["Greedy: 4, then 1, then 1 = 3 coins.","Better: 3 + 3 = 2 coins.","So greedy is not optimal."],"ans":"no: 3 coins vs 2 coins","mistake":"Testing only the amounts where greedy happens to win."},{"t":"ex","level":"exam","title":"[ADDED] Doubling puzzle (PYQ style)","problem":"Start at $x=1$. A step is $+1$ or $\\times2$. Fewest steps to reach 725.","asked":"Number of steps.","idea":"Work **backwards**: if the target is even halve it, if odd subtract 1.","steps":["725 odd: subtract 1 → 724 (1 step).","724→362→181 (halve twice: 3 steps so far).","181→180→90→45→44→22→11→10→5→4→2→1.","Count all moves; a BFS in code confirms the total is **14**."],"ans":"14","mistake":"Going forward greedily (always doubling) overshoots the target."},{"t":"table","head":["Common mistake","Correction"],"rows":[["\"greedy is always optimal\"","false: it needs a proof"],["\"Bellman-Ford is greedy\"","it is dynamic programming (it relaxes all edges repeatedly)"],["testing one example and calling it proven","one example proves nothing for correctness; one counter-example disproves"],["choosing the greedy rule by taste","test it on a tiny input that tries to break it"]]},{"t":"co","kind":"tip","md":"**Mnemonic \"Greedy needs a Guarantee\".** Before trusting a greedy rule, try to break it with a tiny input.","title":"Memory trick"},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"**Exchange argument (how greedy is proved).** Take any optimal answer $O$. If it differs from the greedy answer $G$ at the first step, swap $O$'s choice for the greedy choice and show the answer is **no worse**. Repeat; you turn $O$ into $G$ without losing anything, so $G$ is optimal too.","title":"Why a greedy rule can be trusted"},{"t":"table","head":["Step of the argument","Activity selection example"],"rows":[["1. take an optimal schedule $O$","$O$ starts with some activity $x$"],["2. greedy picks $g$ with the earliest finish","$f(g)\\le f(x)$"],["3. swap $x$ for $g$ in $O$","$g$ ends no later, so it still fits before the rest"],["4. the size is unchanged","still optimal, now agrees with greedy on step 1"],["5. repeat for the remaining activities","$O$ becomes the greedy schedule"]]},{"t":"ex","level":"exam","title":"[ADDED] Build a counter-example yourself","problem":"Rule: \"pay with the largest coin first\". Coins {1, 5, 6, 9}. Find an amount where greedy is not optimal.","asked":"An amount and the two coin counts.","idea":"Try amounts just above a large coin.","steps":["x=11: greedy 9+1+1 = 3 coins; 6+5 = 2 coins.","So x = 11 is a counter-example."],"ans":"x = 11 (greedy 3, optimal 2).","mistake":"Testing only amounts that are themselves coins."},{"t":"ex","level":"tricky","title":"[ADDED] Greedy that **is** optimal for coins","problem":"Indian coins {1, 2, 5, 10}. Is largest-first optimal for every amount up to 100?","asked":"Yes or no, checked.","idea":"Compare greedy with dynamic programming for every amount.","steps":["Checked every amount 1..100 by program.","Greedy count equals the DP minimum each time."],"ans":"Yes. Such coin systems are called canonical.","mistake":"Assuming greedy fails for every coin set."}],"remember":["Greedy = locally best, never undone. Fast, not always correct.","One counter-example disproves a greedy rule.","Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall are DP, not greedy."],"quiz":[{"id":"gr-q1","concept":"Truth","type":"MCQ","stem":"The greedy technique always gives an optimal solution.","options":["True","False"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"Coin change with coins {1,3,4} is a counter-example.","why":{"A":"","B":"Correct."},"sec":"idea"},{"id":"gr-q2","concept":"Coins","type":"SA","stem":"Coins {1,3,4}, pay 6. Optimal number of coins?","num":{"ans":2,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"3 + 3.","sec":"idea"},{"id":"gr-q3","concept":"DP or greedy","type":"MCQ","stem":"Which is a dynamic programming algorithm?","options":["Dijkstra","Prim","Floyd-Warshall","Huffman"],"key":[2],"diff":"easy","explain":"Floyd-Warshall builds on subproblems.","why":{"A":"","B":"","C":"Correct.","D":""},"sec":"idea"},{"id":"gr-q20","concept":"Counter-example","type":"SA","stem":"Coins {1, 5, 6, 9}, amount 11. How many coins does greedy (largest first) use?","num":{"ans":3,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"9 + 1 + 1.","sec":"idea"},{"id":"gr-q21","concept":"Proof","type":"MCQ","stem":"The standard way to prove a greedy rule optimal is","options":["dynamic programming table","exchange argument","brute force on one example","sorting"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"Swap an optimal solution step by step into the greedy one.","why":{"A":"not a proof of greedy","B":"Correct.","C":"one example never proves \"always\"","D":"a tool, not a proof"},"sec":"idea"}]},{"id":"huff","title":"Huffman coding: shorter codes for common symbols","studying":"How to compress text with variable-length prefix codes and how to build the code with a min-heap.","why":"11 PYQs, 35 marks: the largest greedy idea. They ask code lengths, total bits, validity of codes and properties of the tree.","plain":"Give frequent letters short codes and rare letters long codes, using a tree built by repeatedly merging the two smallest weights.","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"A computer normally stores each character with the same number of bits (**fixed-length code**), say 3 bits for up to 8 symbols. But some letters appear far more often than others. If the frequent letters had shorter codes, the message would need fewer bits in total. This is **variable-length coding**."},{"t":"co","kind":"def","md":"**Prefix property.** In a prefix code **no code word is the beginning of another code word**. Then a stream of bits can be decoded without separators: as soon as the bits read so far match a code, that symbol is done. Huffman coding always gives a prefix code.","title":"Prefix code"},{"t":"table","head":["Code set","Prefix-free?","Reason"],"rows":[["A:0, B:1, C:01, D:10","**no**","0 is a prefix of 01; 1 is a prefix of 10"],["A:0, B:10, C:110, D:111","yes","no code starts another"],["A:1, B:01, C:001, D:000","yes","all end where they split"],["A:00, B:01, C:10, D:11","yes","fixed length 2: never a prefix of another"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Why does a tree give a prefix code?** Put the symbols at the **leaves** of a binary tree. A code is the path from the root: left = 0, right = 1. Two leaves can never lie on the same root-to-leaf path, so no code is a prefix of another."},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"**Huffman algorithm.** (1) Make a one-node tree for every symbol with its frequency as weight. (2) Take the **two trees of smallest weight**, join them under a new node whose weight is the sum. (3) Put the new tree back. (4) Repeat until one tree remains. Read codes: left 0, right 1. A symbol's code length = its depth.","title":"Algorithm"},{"t":"code","code":"import heapq\n\ndef huffman_lengths(freq):               # freq: {symbol: frequency}\n    heap = [(f, i, s) for i, (s, f) in enumerate(freq.items())]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)                  # min-heap of (weight, tiebreak, tree)\n    count = len(heap)\n    while len(heap) > 1:\n        f1, _, t1 = heapq.heappop(heap)  # smallest\n        f2, _, t2 = heapq.heappop(heap)  # second smallest\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (f1 + f2, count, (t1, t2)))\n        count += 1\n    return heap[0][2]                    # the finished tree","lex":[["4","Each entry is (weight, tie-break number, tree). The tie-break number keeps the comparison well defined."],["5","A min-heap makes \"take the two smallest\" cost $O(\\log n)$."],["7","Pop the smallest two trees."],["9","Push their merge with the summed weight."],["10","The counter is only a tie-breaker."]],"title":"Huffman with a min-heap"},{"t":"p","md":"**Complexity.** There are $n-1$ merges, each doing two pops and one push at $O(\\log n)$: total $O(n\\log n)$."},{"t":"frames","title":"Huffman on A:50, B:20, C:2, D:10","frames":[{"text":"Start: every symbol is its own one-node tree. Queue (smallest first): C:2, D:10, B:20, A:50."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"12","l":{"v":"C","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"D","s":"10"}}},"text":"**Step 1.** Take the two smallest: **C (2)** and **D (10)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **12**. Queue now: *:12, B:20, A:50."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"12","l":{"v":"C","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"D","s":"10"}},"r":{"v":"B","s":"20"}}},"text":"**Step 2.** Take the two smallest: **subtree (12)** and **B (20)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **32**. Queue now: *:32, A:50."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"82","l":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"12","l":{"v":"C","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"D","s":"10"}},"r":{"v":"B","s":"20"}},"r":{"v":"A","s":"50"}}},"text":"**Step 3.** Take the two smallest: **subtree (32)** and **A (50)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **82**. Queue now: *:82."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"82","l":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"12","l":{"v":"C","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"D","s":"10"}},"r":{"v":"B","s":"20"}},"r":{"v":"A","s":"50"}}},"text":"Done: one tree remains. Go left = 0, right = 1 to read the codes. Depth of a leaf = its code length."}]},{"t":"table","head":["symbol","frequency","code","length","bits used (freq x length)"],"rows":[["A","50","1","1","50"],["B","20","01","2","40"],["C","2","000","3","6"],["D","10","001","3","30"],["total","82","","","126"]]},{"t":"p","md":"A fixed-length code for 4 symbols uses 2 bits each: $82\\cdot2=164$ bits. Huffman uses **126** bits, a saving of $38/164\\approx23\\%$. [ADDED] "},{"t":"co","kind":"notice","md":"**Two different questions.** \"Total bits for the whole message\" = $\\sum f_i\\cdot\\ell_i$ (frequency times code length). \"Total bits to write each symbol once\" (as in the PYQ \"message ABCDEF\") = $\\sum \\ell_i$ (just the lengths).","title":"Which sum?"},{"t":"frames","title":"Huffman on A:13, B:30, C:6, D:35, E:2, F:11","frames":[{"text":"Start: every symbol is its own one-node tree. Queue (smallest first): E:2, C:6, F:11, A:13, B:30, D:35."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}}},"text":"**Step 1.** Take the two smallest: **E (2)** and **C (6)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **8**. Queue now: *:8, F:11, A:13, B:30, D:35."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"19","l":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}},"r":{"v":"F","s":"11"}}},"text":"**Step 2.** Take the two smallest: **subtree (8)** and **F (11)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **19**. Queue now: A:13, *:19, B:30, D:35."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"A","s":"13"},"r":{"v":"19","l":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}},"r":{"v":"F","s":"11"}}}},"text":"**Step 3.** Take the two smallest: **A (13)** and **subtree (19)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **32**. Queue now: B:30, *:32, D:35."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"62","l":{"v":"B","s":"30"},"r":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"A","s":"13"},"r":{"v":"19","l":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}},"r":{"v":"F","s":"11"}}}}},"text":"**Step 4.** Take the two smallest: **B (30)** and **subtree (32)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **62**. Queue now: D:35, *:62."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"97","l":{"v":"D","s":"35"},"r":{"v":"62","l":{"v":"B","s":"30"},"r":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"A","s":"13"},"r":{"v":"19","l":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}},"r":{"v":"F","s":"11"}}}}}},"text":"**Step 5.** Take the two smallest: **D (35)** and **subtree (62)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **97**. Queue now: *:97."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"97","l":{"v":"D","s":"35"},"r":{"v":"62","l":{"v":"B","s":"30"},"r":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"A","s":"13"},"r":{"v":"19","l":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}},"r":{"v":"F","s":"11"}}}}}},"text":"Done: one tree remains. Go left = 0, right = 1 to read the codes. Depth of a leaf = its code length."}]},{"t":"trace","head":["symbol","A","B","C","D","E","F"],"rows":[["frequency","13","30","6","35","2","11"],["code length","3","2","5","1","5","4"]],"title":"Result: A-3, B-2, C-5, D-1, E-5, F-4","stepper":false,"noteLast":false},{"t":"p","md":"**Ties.** When two weights tie, different choices can give **different code lengths** for individual symbols, but the **total** $\\sum f_i\\ell_i$ is the same for every valid Huffman tree. Example: weights 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 80 (one PYQ) has a tie between the symbol C (50) and the merged node (50). Choosing the original leaf first gives total code length 16; choosing the merged node first gives 17. The weighted total bits are equal both ways."},{"t":"table","head":["Property","True?","Reason"],"rows":[["higher-frequency symbols are closer to the root","**true**","merged last"],["lower-frequency symbols are closer to the root","false","merged first, so they end deeper"],["the tree is a strict binary tree (every internal node has 2 children)","**true**","every merge makes a node with exactly 2 children"],["the tree is a complete binary tree","false","depths can differ a lot"],["it generates prefix codes","**true**","symbols are leaves"],["the two lowest-frequency symbols have equal code length (distinct frequencies)","**true**","they are merged first, so siblings"],["there is always a symbol with code length 1","false","four equal frequencies give all codes of length 2"]]},{"t":"ex","level":"simple","title":"[ADDED] Smallest example","problem":"Frequencies a:5, b:9, c:12. Huffman codes lengths and total bits.","asked":"Lengths and total.","idea":"Merge the two smallest.","steps":["Merge 5 and 9 → 14.","Merge 12 and 14 → 26.","c is at depth 1; a and b at depth 2.","Total $=12\\cdot1+5\\cdot2+9\\cdot2=40$ bits."],"ans":"c:1, a:2, b:2, total 40","mistake":"Giving c length 2 because \"it is not the smallest\"."},{"t":"ex","level":"exam","title":"[ADDED] Percentage saved (PYQ style)","problem":"1000 characters, fixed 4 bits each. Huffman average 3 bits. Percentage saved?","asked":"A percentage.","idea":"Compare totals.","steps":["Before: $1000\\cdot4=4000$ bits. After: $1000\\cdot3=3000$ bits.","Saved 1000 bits.","$1000/4000=25\\%$."],"ans":"25 percent","mistake":"Dividing by 3 instead of 4: the base is the **original** size."},{"t":"table","head":["Common mistake","Correction"],"rows":[["merging the two **largest**","always the two smallest"],["adding 0 and 1 wrongly so lengths differ from depths","length = depth of the leaf"],["mixing \"sum of lengths\" with \"sum of freq x length\"","read what the question asks"],["saying the Huffman tree is complete","it is strict (full), not complete"]]},{"t":"co","kind":"tip","md":"**Mnemonic \"Small-Small-Sum\".** Take the two Smallest, make a Sum, push it back. Frequent = near the root = short code.","title":"Memory trick"},{"t":"frames","title":"[ADDED] Classic 6-symbol example (a:5, b:9, c:12, d:13, e:16, f:45)","frames":[{"text":"Start: every symbol is its own one-node tree. Queue (smallest first): a:5, b:9, c:12, d:13, e:16, f:45."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"14","l":{"v":"a","s":"5"},"r":{"v":"b","s":"9"}}},"text":"**Step 1.** Take the two smallest: **a (5)** and **b (9)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **14**. Queue now: c:12, d:13, *:14, e:16, f:45."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"25","l":{"v":"c","s":"12"},"r":{"v":"d","s":"13"}}},"text":"**Step 2.** Take the two smallest: **c (12)** and **d (13)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **25**. Queue now: *:14, e:16, *:25, f:45."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"30","l":{"v":"14","l":{"v":"a","s":"5"},"r":{"v":"b","s":"9"}},"r":{"v":"e","s":"16"}}},"text":"**Step 3.** Take the two smallest: **subtree (14)** and **e (16)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **30**. Queue now: *:25, *:30, f:45."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"55","l":{"v":"25","l":{"v":"c","s":"12"},"r":{"v":"d","s":"13"}},"r":{"v":"30","l":{"v":"14","l":{"v":"a","s":"5"},"r":{"v":"b","s":"9"}},"r":{"v":"e","s":"16"}}}},"text":"**Step 4.** Take the two smallest: **subtree (25)** and **subtree (30)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **55**. Queue now: f:45, *:55."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"100","l":{"v":"f","s":"45"},"r":{"v":"55","l":{"v":"25","l":{"v":"c","s":"12"},"r":{"v":"d","s":"13"}},"r":{"v":"30","l":{"v":"14","l":{"v":"a","s":"5"},"r":{"v":"b","s":"9"}},"r":{"v":"e","s":"16"}}}}},"text":"**Step 5.** Take the two smallest: **f (45)** and **subtree (55)**. Merge them into a new node of weight **100**. Queue now: *:100."},{"fig":{"tree":{"v":"100","l":{"v":"f","s":"45"},"r":{"v":"55","l":{"v":"25","l":{"v":"c","s":"12"},"r":{"v":"d","s":"13"}},"r":{"v":"30","l":{"v":"14","l":{"v":"a","s":"5"},"r":{"v":"b","s":"9"}},"r":{"v":"e","s":"16"}}}}},"text":"Done: one tree remains. Go left = 0, right = 1 to read the codes. Depth of a leaf = its code length."}]},{"t":"table","head":["symbol","frequency","code length","bits"],"rows":[["a","5","4","20"],["b","9","4","36"],["c","12","3","36"],["d","13","3","39"],["e","16","3","48"],["f","45","1","45"],["total","100","","224"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Fixed-length coding of 6 symbols needs 3 bits each: $100\\cdot3=300$ bits. Huffman needs **224** bits, a saving of $76/300\\approx25\\%$. [ADDED] "},{"t":"co","kind":"tip","md":"**Shortcut for total bits:** add up every merge weight. Here $14+25+30+55+100=224$. Each symbol's frequency is added once per level it sits below, which is exactly frequency × depth.","title":"Check your answer in 10 seconds"},{"t":"ex","level":"exam","title":"[ADDED] Average code length","problem":"Probabilities 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1. Average bits per symbol with Huffman?","asked":"A number.","idea":"Build the tree, then $\\sum p_i\\ell_i$.","steps":["Merge 0.1+0.2 = 0.3; then 0.3+0.3 = 0.6; then 0.4+0.6 = 1.","Lengths: 0.4 → 1, 0.3 → 2, 0.2 → 3, 0.1 → 3.","Average $=0.4+0.6+0.6+0.3=1.9$."],"ans":"1.9 bits per symbol (versus 2 for fixed length).","mistake":"Using the sum of lengths (9) instead of the weighted average."},{"t":"ex","level":"tricky","title":"[ADDED] A tie that changes one length","problem":"Frequencies 1, 1, 2, 2. Can the code lengths be (2, 2, 2, 2) and also (3, 3, 2, 1)?","asked":"Yes or no for each.","idea":"Ties allow different trees with the same total.","steps":["Merge the two 1s → 2. Now three weights tie at 2: the two leaves and the new node.","Choice 1: merge the two **leaves** (2+2 = 4), then 2+4. Every symbol ends at depth 2: lengths 2, 2, 2, 2. Total $2(1+1+2+2)=12$.","Choice 2: merge a leaf with the **new node** (2+2 = 4), then the other leaf with 4. Lengths: 1s at depth 3, one 2 at depth 2, the other 2 at depth 1. Total $1\\cdot3+1\\cdot3+2\\cdot2+2\\cdot1=12$."],"ans":"Both shapes are valid Huffman trees; the total (12 bits) is the same.","mistake":"Thinking only one set of lengths is correct."}],"remember":["Merge the two smallest weights, repeat. Depth = code length.","Total bits = sum of frequency x length.","Tree is strict binary; codes are prefix-free; two rarest are siblings."],"quiz":[{"id":"gr-q4","concept":"Total","type":"SA","stem":"Frequencies 3, 5, 6, 8: total Huffman bits (sum of frequency x length)?","num":{"ans":44,"tol":0},"diff":"hard","explain":"Merges: 3+5=8. The queue is now 6, 8, 8: merge 6 with one of the 8s → 14. Finally 8+14=22. The total weighted cost equals the sum of merge weights: 8+14+22=44. Every symbol gets length 2.","sec":"huff"},{"id":"gr-q5","concept":"Prefix","type":"MCQ","stem":"Which set is **not** a prefix code?","options":["0, 10, 11","1, 01, 001","0, 01, 11","00, 01, 10"],"key":[2],"diff":"easy","explain":"0 is a prefix of 01.","why":{"A":"","B":"","C":"Correct.","D":""},"sec":"huff"},{"id":"gr-q6","concept":"Tree","type":"MCQ","stem":"The Huffman tree is always:","options":["complete","strict binary","a BST","balanced"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"Every internal node has two children.","why":{"A":"","B":"Correct.","C":"","D":""},"sec":"huff"},{"id":"gr-q22","concept":"Total","type":"SA","stem":"Frequencies 5, 9, 12, 13, 16, 45: total Huffman bits?","num":{"ans":224,"tol":0},"diff":"medium","explain":"Sum of merges 14+25+30+55+100.","sec":"huff"},{"id":"gr-q23","concept":"Average","type":"SA","stem":"Probabilities 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1: average Huffman code length (one decimal)?","num":{"ans":1.9,"tol":0},"diff":"medium","explain":"0.4·1+0.3·2+0.2·3+0.1·3.","sec":"huff"}]},{"id":"ivl","title":"Interval scheduling and meeting rooms","studying":"Choosing the most non-overlapping activities with one room, and the fewest rooms for all meetings.","why":"9 PYQs, 31 marks. All are computations with a clear method, so they are reliable marks.","plain":"One room: sort by finish time and take whatever fits. Many rooms: count the maximum number of meetings happening at once.","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"**Problem 1: activity selection.** We have one room and a list of activities `(start, finish)`. Two activities are compatible if one finishes at or before the other starts (the PYQs say \"finish at $T$, next may start at $T$\"). Choose as many compatible activities as possible."},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"**Rule: earliest finish time first.** Sort by finish time. Take the first activity. Then walk down the list and take each activity whose start is $\\ge$ the finish time of the last one taken.","title":"Greedy rule"},{"t":"p","md":"**Why earliest finish?** The activity that finishes first leaves the **most room** for the remaining ones. Exchange argument: take an optimal schedule; its first activity finishes no earlier than ours. Replace it with ours: still compatible with the rest, and the count is unchanged. So some optimal schedule contains our choice."},{"t":"table","head":["Rule","Works?","Counter-example"],"rows":[["earliest start first","no","(0,10) and (1,2), (3,4), (5,6): picks (0,10) first, only 1 activity instead of 3"],["shortest duration first","no","(1,6), (5,8), (7,12): the middle one is shortest but blocks two; best is 2 activities, shortest gives 1"],["fewest conflicts first","no (not needed)","skip"],["**earliest finish first**","**yes**","proved by the exchange argument"]]},{"t":"code","code":"def max_activities(acts):\n    acts.sort(key=lambda a: a[1])     # by finish time\n    chosen, last_end = [], float(\"-inf\")\n    for start, end in acts:\n        if start >= last_end:          # compatible: starts at or after the last finish\n            chosen.append((start, end))\n            last_end = end\n    return chosen","lex":[["2","Sort by finish time: $O(n\\log n)$."],["3","`last_end` is the finish time of the last chosen activity."],["5","`>=` (not `>`): the PYQs allow starting exactly when the previous one ends."],["7","Update the finish time."]],"title":"activity selection"},{"t":"fig","timeline":{"items":[{"name":"A1","s":8,"e":11,"hl":true},{"name":"A2","s":1,"e":4,"hl":true},{"name":"A3","s":3,"e":5,"hl":false},{"name":"A4","s":0,"e":6,"hl":false},{"name":"A5","s":5,"e":7,"hl":true},{"name":"A6","s":3,"e":9,"hl":false},{"name":"A7","s":5,"e":9,"hl":false},{"name":"A8","s":6,"e":10,"hl":false},{"name":"A9","s":8,"e":12,"hl":false},{"name":"A10","s":12,"e":14,"hl":true}],"title":"Activity selection"},"caption":"PYQ activities A1..A10 (Aug 2026). The chosen ones (A2, A5, A1, A10) are highlighted."},{"t":"trace","head":["activity (sorted by finish)","start","finish","finish of last chosen","take?"],"rows":[["A2","1","4","-","yes"],["A3","3","5","4","no (3 < 4)"],["A4","0","6","4","no (0 < 4)"],["A5","5","7","4","yes"],["A6","3","9","7","no (3 < 7)"],["A7","5","9","7","no (5 < 7)"],["A8","6","10","7","no (6 < 7)"],["A1","8","11","7","yes"],["A9","8","12","11","no (8 < 11)"],["A10","12","14","11","yes"]],"title":"Walk through the PYQ list","stepper":true,"noteLast":false,"foot":"Chosen: A1, A2, A5, A10. Count = 4."},{"t":"p","md":"**Complexity.** Sorting dominates: $O(n\\log n)$. The scan is $O(n)$."},{"t":"p","md":"**Problem 2: minimum number of rooms.** Every meeting needs a room, and meetings that overlap need different rooms. The answer is the **maximum number of meetings in progress at the same time**."},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"**Sweep method.** List every start as $+1$ and every end as $-1$. Sort by time; at the **same time put ends before starts** (because a meeting ending at $t$ frees its room for one starting at $t$). Walk through, keep a running total. The largest total is the number of rooms.","title":"Sweep method"},{"t":"code","code":"import heapq\n\ndef min_rooms(meetings):\n    meetings.sort()                 # by start time\n    ends = []                       # min-heap of end times of rooms in use\n    for start, end in meetings:\n        if ends and ends[0] <= start:\n            heapq.heapreplace(ends, end)   # reuse the room that frees first\n        else:\n            heapq.heappush(ends, end)      # need a new room\n    return len(ends)","lex":[["2","Process meetings in order of start time."],["3","`ends` holds, for every room, when it becomes free. The smallest is at index 0."],["5","If the earliest-free room is free by `start`, reuse it (`<=` because end = start is allowed)."],["7","Otherwise open a new room."],["8","Number of rooms = size of the heap."]],"title":"meeting rooms with a min-heap"},{"t":"ex","level":"exam","title":"[ADDED] Rooms by sweeping","problem":"Meetings `(1,4), (6,12), (2,8), (11,15), (3,7), (5,10), (9,14), (13,16)` (PYQ Mar 2024).","asked":"Minimum rooms.","idea":"Count overlaps at the busiest moment.","steps":["At time 6.5: (2,8), (3,7), (6,12), (5,10) are all running: 4 meetings.","No moment has 5 running. By the sweep program: maximum = 4."],"ans":"4","mistake":"Counting rooms as the number of meetings that overlap with any one meeting. It must overlap at the **same instant**."},{"t":"trace","head":["time","event","meetings running"],"rows":[["1","+(1,4)","1"],["2","+(2,8)","2"],["3","+(3,7)","3"],["4","-(1,4)","2"],["5","+(5,10)","3"],["6","+(6,12)","4"],["7","-(3,7)","3"],["8","-(2,8)","2"],["9","+(9,14)","3"],["10","-(5,10)","2"],["11","+(11,15)","3"],["12","-(6,12)","2"],["13","+(13,16)","3"]],"title":"Sweep for the PYQ list: maximum 4 at time 6","stepper":true,"noteLast":false},{"t":"table","head":["Common mistake","Correction"],"rows":[["using `>` so an activity starting at the end time is rejected","the PYQs say starting at $T$ is allowed: use `>=`"],["sorting by start for activity selection","sort by **finish**"],["counting rooms as the number of meetings","count simultaneous meetings only"],["giving the sweep start before end at the same time","ends first, so room reuse at the same time works"]]},{"t":"co","kind":"tip","md":"**Mnemonic \"Finish Fast, Fit Many\".** One room: earliest finish. Many rooms: busiest instant.","title":"Memory trick"},{"t":"frames","title":"[ADDED] Earliest-finish selection, step by step","frames":[{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"P","s":1,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":null},{"name":"U","s":3,"e":4,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"Q","s":2,"e":5,"row":2,"hl":null},{"name":"R","s":4,"e":7,"row":3,"hl":null},{"name":"S","s":6,"e":9,"row":4,"hl":null},{"name":"T","s":8,"e":10,"row":5,"hl":null}],"title":"Sorted by finish time (top = earliest finish)","rowLabel":""}},"text":"**Start.** Sort the activities by **finish time**. Nothing is chosen yet; the last chosen finish is $-\\infty$."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"P","s":1,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"U","s":3,"e":4,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"Q","s":2,"e":5,"row":2,"hl":null},{"name":"R","s":4,"e":7,"row":3,"hl":null},{"name":"S","s":6,"e":9,"row":4,"hl":null},{"name":"T","s":8,"e":10,"row":5,"hl":null}],"title":"Earliest-finish scan"}},"text":"**P (1, 3):** start 1 ≥ last finish −∞, so **take it** (green). Last finish becomes 3."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"P","s":1,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"U","s":3,"e":4,"row":1,"hl":true},{"name":"Q","s":2,"e":5,"row":2,"hl":null},{"name":"R","s":4,"e":7,"row":3,"hl":null},{"name":"S","s":6,"e":9,"row":4,"hl":null},{"name":"T","s":8,"e":10,"row":5,"hl":null}],"title":"Earliest-finish scan"}},"text":"**U (3, 4):** start 3 ≥ last finish 3, so **take it** (green). Last finish becomes 4."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"P","s":1,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"U","s":3,"e":4,"row":1,"hl":true},{"name":"Q","s":2,"e":5,"row":2,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"R","s":4,"e":7,"row":3,"hl":null},{"name":"S","s":6,"e":9,"row":4,"hl":null},{"name":"T","s":8,"e":10,"row":5,"hl":null}],"title":"Earliest-finish scan"}},"text":"**Q (2, 5):** start 2 < last finish 4, so it overlaps: **skip** (red)."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"P","s":1,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"U","s":3,"e":4,"row":1,"hl":true},{"name":"Q","s":2,"e":5,"row":2,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"R","s":4,"e":7,"row":3,"hl":true},{"name":"S","s":6,"e":9,"row":4,"hl":null},{"name":"T","s":8,"e":10,"row":5,"hl":null}],"title":"Earliest-finish scan"}},"text":"**R (4, 7):** start 4 ≥ last finish 4, so **take it** (green). Last finish becomes 7."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"P","s":1,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"U","s":3,"e":4,"row":1,"hl":true},{"name":"Q","s":2,"e":5,"row":2,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"R","s":4,"e":7,"row":3,"hl":true},{"name":"S","s":6,"e":9,"row":4,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"T","s":8,"e":10,"row":5,"hl":null}],"title":"Earliest-finish scan"}},"text":"**S (6, 9):** start 6 < last finish 7, so it overlaps: **skip** (red)."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"P","s":1,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"U","s":3,"e":4,"row":1,"hl":true},{"name":"Q","s":2,"e":5,"row":2,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"R","s":4,"e":7,"row":3,"hl":true},{"name":"S","s":6,"e":9,"row":4,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"T","s":8,"e":10,"row":5,"hl":true}],"title":"Earliest-finish scan"}},"text":"**T (8, 10):** start 8 ≥ last finish 7, so **take it** (green). Last finish becomes 10."},{"text":"**Done.** Chosen: P, U, R, T, so **4** activities."}]},{"t":"frames","title":"[ADDED] Minimum rooms: each meeting goes to a free room or opens a new one","frames":[{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":0,"e":4,"row":0,"hl":true}],"title":"Rooms in use","rowLabel":"room","tmin":0,"tmax":9}},"text":"**M1 (0, 4):** every room is busy at time 0, so **open room 1**."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":0,"e":4,"row":0,"hl":null},{"name":"M2","s":1,"e":3,"row":1,"hl":true}],"title":"Rooms in use","rowLabel":"room","tmin":0,"tmax":9}},"text":"**M2 (1, 3):** every room is busy at time 1, so **open room 2**."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":0,"e":4,"row":0,"hl":null},{"name":"M2","s":1,"e":3,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"M3","s":2,"e":6,"row":2,"hl":true}],"title":"Rooms in use","rowLabel":"room","tmin":0,"tmax":9}},"text":"**M3 (2, 6):** every room is busy at time 2, so **open room 3**."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":0,"e":4,"row":0,"hl":null},{"name":"M2","s":1,"e":3,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"M3","s":2,"e":6,"row":2,"hl":null},{"name":"M6","s":3,"e":5,"row":1,"hl":true}],"title":"Rooms in use","rowLabel":"room","tmin":0,"tmax":9}},"text":"**M6 (3, 5):** room 2 is free (it ended at 3 ≤ 3), reuse it."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":0,"e":4,"row":0,"hl":null},{"name":"M2","s":1,"e":3,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"M3","s":2,"e":6,"row":2,"hl":null},{"name":"M6","s":3,"e":5,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"M4","s":5,"e":8,"row":0,"hl":true}],"title":"Rooms in use","rowLabel":"room","tmin":0,"tmax":9}},"text":"**M4 (5, 8):** room 1 is free (it ended at 4 ≤ 5), reuse it."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":0,"e":4,"row":0,"hl":null},{"name":"M2","s":1,"e":3,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"M3","s":2,"e":6,"row":2,"hl":null},{"name":"M6","s":3,"e":5,"row":1,"hl":null},{"name":"M4","s":5,"e":8,"row":0,"hl":null},{"name":"M5","s":7,"e":9,"row":1,"hl":true}],"title":"Rooms in use","rowLabel":"room","tmin":0,"tmax":9}},"text":"**M5 (7, 9):** room 2 is free (it ended at 5 ≤ 7), reuse it."},{"text":"**Done.** 3 rooms were opened, which equals the largest number of meetings running at the same moment."}]},{"t":"co","kind":"notice","md":"**Why rooms = peak overlap.** At the busiest moment $k$ meetings run together, so you need at least $k$ rooms. The room-filling procedure above never opens room $k+1$ unless $k+1$ meetings overlap. Lower bound = what the algorithm uses, so it is optimal.","title":"The two-sided argument"},{"t":"table","head":["Question wording","Which method","Answer type"],"rows":[["\"maximum number of activities / meetings in one room\"","earliest-finish scan","a count of chosen activities"],["\"minimum number of rooms / halls / machines\"","sweep for the peak overlap","a count of rooms"],["\"which greedy strategy is optimal\"","earliest finish","a rule"],["events already given in time order","running count +1 / −1","the peak"]]},{"t":"ex","level":"exam","title":"[ADDED] Ends exactly when the next starts","problem":"Activities (1,3), (3,5), (5,7). Maximum activities if a new one may start at the finish time?","asked":"A count.","idea":"Compatibility uses ≥.","steps":["Sorted: (1,3), (3,5), (5,7).","3 ≥ 3, take; 5 ≥ 5, take."],"ans":"3","mistake":"Using > and getting 1."}],"remember":["Max activities: sort by finish, take if start >= last finish.","Min rooms: maximum simultaneous meetings (ends before starts at ties).","Earliest start and shortest first are wrong."],"quiz":[{"id":"gr-q7","concept":"Select","type":"SA","stem":"Activities (1,3), (2,4), (3,5), (4,6): maximum number compatible (end = start allowed)?","num":{"ans":2,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"(1,3) and (3,5), or (2,4) and (4,6): 2.","sec":"ivl"},{"id":"gr-q8","concept":"Rule","type":"MCQ","stem":"Which rule is optimal for maximizing the number of activities?","options":["earliest start","earliest finish","shortest","longest"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"Leaves the most room.","why":{"A":"","B":"Correct.","C":"","D":""},"sec":"ivl"},{"id":"gr-q9","concept":"Rooms","type":"SA","stem":"Meetings (0,5), (1,3), (2,4), (6,8): rooms needed?","num":{"ans":3,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"At time 2.5: (0,5), (1,3), (2,4).","sec":"ivl"},{"id":"gr-q24","concept":"Select","type":"SA","stem":"Activities (1,3), (2,5), (4,7), (6,9), (8,10), (3,4): maximum number?","num":{"ans":4,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Earliest finish: (1,3), (3,4), (4,7), (8,10).","sec":"ivl"},{"id":"gr-q25","concept":"Rooms","type":"SA","stem":"Meetings (0,4), (1,3), (2,6), (5,8), (7,9), (3,5): minimum rooms?","num":{"ans":3,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Peak overlap.","sec":"ivl"}]},{"id":"late","title":"Minimizing the maximum lateness","studying":"Ordering jobs on one machine so that the worst delay is as small as possible.","why":"3 PYQs, 9 marks: one asks which greedy rule is correct, two compute the minimum lateness.","plain":"Do the job with the earliest deadline first.","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"There are $n$ jobs, each with a processing time $T_i$ and a deadline $D_i$. One job runs at a time, with no gaps, starting at time 0. If job $i$ finishes at $F_i$, its **lateness** is $L_i=\\max(0,F_i-D_i)$. We want an order that makes the **largest** lateness $M_L=\\max_i L_i$ as small as possible."},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"**Rule: Earliest Deadline First (EDF).** Sort jobs by deadline, smallest first. Run them in that order with no idle time.","title":"Greedy rule"},{"t":"table","head":["Rule","Correct?","Why or counter-example"],"rows":[["earliest deadline first","**yes**","exchange argument below"],["shortest processing time first","no","jobs (T=1,D=100) and (T=2,D=2): shortest-first runs the long-deadline job first, so the urgent one finishes at 3 > 2"],["smallest slack $D_i-T_i$ first","no","X(T=2,D=10) has slack 8 and Y(T=9,D=11) has slack 2. Slack-first runs Y then X: X ends at 11, lateness 1. EDF runs X then Y: both on time, lateness 0"],["longest first","no","no logic"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Check the slack counter-example by program.** [ADDED] Jobs X(2,10), Y(9,11): the order Y, X gives maximum lateness 1, the order X, Y (EDF) gives 0."},{"t":"p","md":"**Exchange argument.** Suppose a schedule has two neighbouring jobs $i,j$ with $D_i>D_j$ but $i$ runs first (an **inversion**). Swap them. Job $i$ finishes later, but its deadline is the later one; job $j$ now finishes earlier. The worst lateness of the pair does not get worse, so we can remove inversions one at a time until the schedule is in deadline order, without increasing $M_L$."},{"t":"trace","head":["job (in deadline order)","duration $T$","deadline $D$","finish $F$","lateness $L$"],"rows":[["A6","2","5","2","0"],["A1","4","6","6","0"],["A2","2","7","8","1"],["A3","1","8","9","1"],["A4","4","12","13","1"],["A5","3","14","16","2"]],"title":"PYQ Aug 2024: EDF order and lateness","stepper":true,"noteLast":false,"foot":"Maximum lateness = 2, which is the minimum possible (brute force over all 720 orders agrees)."},{"t":"trace","head":["job (in deadline order)","duration $T$","deadline $D$","finish $F$","lateness $L$"],"rows":[["A6","2","2","2","0"],["A2","2","4","4","0"],["A4","4","7","8","1"],["A3","1","9","9","0"],["A7","5","12","14","2"],["A1","4","14","18","4"],["A5","3","19","21","2"]],"title":"PYQ Mar 2025: EDF order and lateness","stepper":true,"noteLast":false,"foot":"Maximum lateness = 4 (brute force over all 5040 orders agrees)."},{"t":"code","code":"def min_max_lateness(jobs):            # jobs: list of (T, D)\n    jobs.sort(key=lambda j: j[1])        # earliest deadline first\n    t, worst = 0, 0\n    for T, D in jobs:\n        t += T                           # finish time of this job\n        worst = max(worst, t - D)        # lateness (0 if early)\n    return worst","lex":[["2","Sort by deadline: $O(n\\log n)$."],["4","No idle time: each job starts when the last one ends."],["6","`worst` starts at 0, so early jobs count as 0 lateness."]],"title":"EDF"},{"t":"ex","level":"exam","title":"[ADDED] Compute minimum lateness","problem":"Jobs $(T,D)$: (3,4), (2,3), (4,10). Minimum possible $M_L$?","asked":"The smallest maximum lateness.","idea":"Sort by deadline, then accumulate.","steps":["Order by deadline: (2,3), (3,4), (4,10).","Finish times 2, 5, 9. Lateness: max(0,2-3)=0; max(0,5-4)=1; max(0,9-10)=0.","Maximum = 1."],"ans":"1","mistake":"Using the order given in the table instead of sorting."},{"t":"table","head":["Common mistake","Correction"],"rows":[["sorting by shortest duration","sort by deadline"],["forgetting that lateness is never negative","early jobs count as 0"],["inserting idle gaps","never idle; it only delays everything"],["not accumulating the finish times","finish = running sum of durations"]]},{"t":"co","kind":"tip","md":"**Mnemonic \"Due Date First\".** The one due soonest goes first.","title":"Memory trick"},{"t":"frames","title":"[ADDED] Earliest deadline first on six jobs","frames":[{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"J1","s":0,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true}],"title":"Schedule so far (one machine)","tmin":0,"tmax":15,"rowLabel":"run"}},"text":"**J1** (T=3, D=6) runs from 0 to **3**. Lateness $=\\max(0,3-6)=0$. Largest lateness so far: 0."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"J1","s":0,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J2","s":3,"e":5,"row":0,"hl":true}],"title":"Schedule so far (one machine)","tmin":0,"tmax":15,"rowLabel":"run"}},"text":"**J2** (T=2, D=8) runs from 3 to **5**. Lateness $=\\max(0,5-8)=0$. Largest lateness so far: 0."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"J1","s":0,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J2","s":3,"e":5,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J3","s":5,"e":6,"row":0,"hl":true}],"title":"Schedule so far (one machine)","tmin":0,"tmax":15,"rowLabel":"run"}},"text":"**J3** (T=1, D=9) runs from 5 to **6**. Lateness $=\\max(0,6-9)=0$. Largest lateness so far: 0."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"J1","s":0,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J2","s":3,"e":5,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J3","s":5,"e":6,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J4","s":6,"e":10,"row":0,"hl":"bad"}],"title":"Schedule so far (one machine)","tmin":0,"tmax":15,"rowLabel":"run"}},"text":"**J4** (T=4, D=9) runs from 6 to **10**. Lateness $=\\max(0,10-9)=1$. Largest lateness so far: 1."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"J1","s":0,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J2","s":3,"e":5,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J3","s":5,"e":6,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J4","s":6,"e":10,"row":0,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"J5","s":10,"e":13,"row":0,"hl":true}],"title":"Schedule so far (one machine)","tmin":0,"tmax":15,"rowLabel":"run"}},"text":"**J5** (T=3, D=14) runs from 10 to **13**. Lateness $=\\max(0,13-14)=0$. Largest lateness so far: 1."},{"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"J1","s":0,"e":3,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J2","s":3,"e":5,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J3","s":5,"e":6,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J4","s":6,"e":10,"row":0,"hl":"bad"},{"name":"J5","s":10,"e":13,"row":0,"hl":true},{"name":"J6","s":13,"e":15,"row":0,"hl":true}],"title":"Schedule so far (one machine)","tmin":0,"tmax":15,"rowLabel":"run"}},"text":"**J6** (T=2, D=15) runs from 13 to **15**. Lateness $=\\max(0,15-15)=0$. Largest lateness so far: 1."},{"text":"**Done.** Maximum lateness **1**; brute force over every order confirms no order does better."}]},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"**Why EDF is optimal (exchange).** If a schedule runs job $j$ right before job $i$ while $d_i<d_j$ (an inversion), swap them. Job $i$ finishes earlier, so its lateness does not grow; job $j$ now finishes when $i$ used to, and since $d_j>d_i$ its lateness is at most $i$'s old lateness. So the maximum never increases. Remove every inversion and you get EDF.","title":"Proof idea"},{"t":"ex","level":"tricky","title":"[ADDED] Idle time never helps","problem":"Jobs (T=2, D=3), (T=1, D=1). Is waiting before starting ever useful?","asked":"Yes or no.","idea":"Delaying a job only makes it finish later.","steps":["EDF runs (1,1) first: finish 1, lateness 0; then (2,3): finish 3, lateness 0.","Any idle gap only pushes finish times later."],"ans":"No: an optimal schedule has no idle time.","mistake":"Inserting gaps to \"wait for\" deadlines."}],"remember":["EDF: earliest deadline first minimizes the maximum lateness.","Finish = running sum; lateness = max(0, finish - deadline).","Shortest-first and smallest-slack are wrong."],"quiz":[{"id":"gr-q10","concept":"Rule","type":"MCQ","stem":"Which rule minimizes the maximum lateness?","options":["shortest job first","earliest deadline first","longest job first","smallest slack first"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"EDF is the proven rule.","why":{"A":"","B":"Correct.","C":"","D":""},"sec":"late"},{"id":"gr-q11","concept":"Compute","type":"SA","stem":"Jobs (T,D): (2,2), (2,4), (2,6). EDF maximum lateness?","num":{"ans":0,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Finish 2, 4, 6 meet deadlines exactly.","sec":"late"},{"id":"gr-q12","concept":"Compute","type":"SA","stem":"Jobs (T,D): (3,3), (3,4). EDF maximum lateness?","num":{"ans":2,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Finish 3, 6; lateness 0 and 2.","sec":"late"},{"id":"gr-q26","concept":"EDF","type":"SA","stem":"Jobs (T,D): (3,6), (2,8), (1,9), (4,9), (3,14), (2,15). Minimum maximum lateness?","num":{"ans":1,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Run in deadline order; finish times 3, 5, 6, 10, 13, 15.","sec":"late"}]},{"id":"hub","title":"Compare, connect and revise: the whole topic on one page","studying":"All four greedy ideas with their rule, cost and counter-examples.","why":"Questions mix them: pick the right rule fast.","plain":"One table of rules, one of traps, and three mnemonics.","blocks":[{"t":"fig","svg":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 700 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"concept map\" style=\"max-width:700px\"><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#2f6fdf\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" opacity=\".7\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"35\" width=\"170\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#2f6fdf 10%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#2f6fdf\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"51\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">Huffman</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">merge two smallest</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">depth = length</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">total = sum f x l</text><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"585\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#1c9a6c\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" opacity=\".7\"/><rect x=\"500\" y=\"35\" width=\"170\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#1c9a6c 10%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#1c9a6c\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><text x=\"585\" y=\"51\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">Intervals</text><text x=\"585\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">earliest finish first</text><text x=\"585\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">rooms = peak overlap</text><text x=\"585\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">start >= last end</text><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"325\" stroke=\"#c9811a\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" opacity=\".7\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"305\" width=\"170\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#c9811a 10%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#c9811a\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><text x=\"115\" y=\"321\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">Lateness</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"336\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">earliest deadline first</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">finish = running sum</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">L = max(0, F - D)</text><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"585\" y2=\"325\" stroke=\"#b04a8f\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" opacity=\".7\"/><rect x=\"500\" y=\"305\" width=\"170\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#b04a8f 10%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#b04a8f\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><text x=\"585\" y=\"321\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">Counter-examples</text><text x=\"585\" y=\"336\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">coins 1,3,4</text><text x=\"585\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">Bellman-Ford is DP</text><text x=\"585\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">one example disproves</text><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"350\" stroke=\"#d0463b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" opacity=\".7\"/><rect x=\"265\" y=\"330\" width=\"170\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"color-mix(in srgb,#d0463b 10%,var(--card))\" stroke=\"#d0463b\" stroke-width=\"1.8\"/><text x=\"350\" y=\"346\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"var(--ink)\">Cost</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"361\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">sort O(n log n)</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">heap O(n log n)</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"389\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"10.5\" fill=\"var(--mute)\">scan O(n)</text><ellipse cx=\"350\" cy=\"200\" rx=\"78\" ry=\"30\" fill=\"#fff1a1\" stroke=\"#c9811a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"350\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#222\">Greedy</text></svg>","caption":"Concept map."},{"t":"table","head":["Problem","Greedy rule","Data structure","Time","Optimal?"],"rows":[["Huffman coding","merge two smallest weights","min-heap","$O(n\\log n)$","yes"],["max activities, one room","earliest finish first","sort","$O(n\\log n)$","yes"],["min meeting rooms","sweep or earliest-free room","min-heap or sort of events","$O(n\\log n)$","yes"],["min max lateness","earliest deadline first","sort","$O(n\\log n)$","yes"],["coin change, any coins","largest coin first","sort","$O(k\\log k)$ sort + one step per coin used","**no**"]]},{"t":"table","head":["Formula or method","Use"],"rows":[["total Huffman bits","$\\sum f_i\\ell_i$ (= sum of merge weights)"],["sum of code lengths","$\\sum\\ell_i$ (message with each symbol once)"],["percent saved","(old - new) / old"],["lateness","$\\max(0,F_i-D_i)$, $F_i$ = running sum"],["rooms","max simultaneous meetings, ends before starts at ties"]]},{"t":"table","head":["Common mistake","Correction"],"rows":[["sorting activities by start","sort by finish"],["using `>` for compatibility","start >= last finish"],["merging the two largest in Huffman","two smallest"],["saying greedy is always optimal","false; coins {1,3,4}"],["saying Bellman-Ford is greedy","dynamic programming"]]},{"t":"co","kind":"tip","md":"Mnemonics: **\"Small-Small-Sum\"** (Huffman). **\"Finish Fast, Fit Many\"** (intervals). **\"Due Date First\"** (lateness).","title":"Remember these"},{"t":"co","kind":"remember","md":"Revision: Huffman merge-smallest; activities earliest finish; lateness earliest deadline; one counter-example kills a greedy rule.","title":"One-line summary"}],"remember":["Four rules: merge smallest, earliest finish, peak overlap, earliest deadline."],"quiz":[{"id":"gr-q15","concept":"Rule","type":"MCQ","stem":"Earliest finish time first is optimal for:","options":["minimum rooms","maximum activities in one room","minimum lateness","Huffman"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"Activity selection.","why":{"A":"","B":"Correct.","C":"","D":""},"sec":"hub"},{"id":"gr-q16","concept":"Drill","type":"SA","stem":"Percent saved: 6 bits fixed, 4.5 bits average?","num":{"ans":25,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"(6-4.5)/6.","sec":"hub"},{"id":"gr-q17","concept":"DP","type":"MCQ","stem":"Which is not greedy?","options":["Huffman","Prim","Bellman-Ford","Dijkstra"],"key":[2],"diff":"easy","explain":"Bellman-Ford is DP.","why":{"A":"","B":"","C":"Correct.","D":""},"sec":"hub"}]}],"patterns":[{"name":"Huffman: lengths, total bits, validity, properties","first":"Merge the two smallest; depth = code length; total = sum of freq x length.","ids":["6406531338315","6406532035104","6406532180383","6406532180385","640653351977","6406531172986","640653690095","6406531021266","640653771120","640653854180","640653586789"]},{"name":"Max activities and minimum rooms","first":"One room: sort by finish. Many rooms: peak simultaneous meetings.","ids":["6406532180384","640653586790","640653690096","6406531172991","6406532035103","640653351978","640653771121","640653771122","6406531021264"]},{"name":"Minimum maximum lateness","first":"Sort by deadline, accumulate finish times.","ids":["6406532035105","640653854181","6406531172987"]},{"name":"Is the greedy rule correct? Counter-examples","first":"Try to break the rule on a tiny input. Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall are not greedy.","ids":["6406531338313","6406531021265","640653854182"]}],"pyqs":[{"id":"6406531338315","exam":"03 Aug 2025","qno":"9","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"Lets consider a message with characters and frequencies as given below.\n\n| Characters | $A$ | $B$ | $C$ | $D$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Frequencies | 50 | 20 | 2 | 10 |\n\nWhat is the total bits required to store above message using the Huffman coding algorithm?","sol":{"asked":"Total bits = $\\sum f_i\\ell_i$.","idea":"Huffman merging, then a weighted sum.","method":"Merge the two smallest each time: 2+10=12, then 12+20=32, then 32+50=82. Depths: A=1, B=2, C=3, D=3.\n\nTotal $=50\\cdot1+20\\cdot2+2\\cdot3+10\\cdot3=50+40+6+30=126$ bits.","opts":{"A":"328 is far too large: even a fixed 2-bit code for 82 symbols needs only 164.","B":"82 is the sum of the frequencies, i.e. the number of characters, not the bits.","C":"100 is not the weighted total of any valid tree here.","D":"**Correct.** 126 bits."},"verify":"Code: lengths {'C': 3, 'D': 3, 'B': 2, 'A': 1}, total 126.","trap":"Answering 82, the number of characters.","rule":"\"Total bits\" with frequencies → $\\sum f_i\\ell_i$ (also equals the sum of all merge weights: 12+32+82 = 126).","transfer":"Fixed-length 2 bits would need 164 bits; the saving is 38 bits.","clue":"Build the Huffman tree, read each symbol's depth, then add frequency x length.","first":"Merge 2 and 10.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["merge","trees merged","new weight"],"rows":[["1","C (2) + D (10)","12"],["2","subtree (12) + B (20)","32"],["3","subtree (32) + A (50)","82"]],"title":"Merges","stepper":true,"noteLast":false},"fig":{"tree":{"v":"82","l":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"12","l":{"v":"C","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"D","s":"10"}},"r":{"v":"B","s":"20"}},"r":{"v":"A","s":"50"}}}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["328","82","100","126"],"key":[3]},{"id":"6406532035104","exam":"06 Apr 2026","qno":"6","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"Suppose the letters $\\{a, b, c, d, e\\}$ occur with the following frequencies.\n\n| Character | a | b | c | d | e |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Frequencies | 26 | 21 | 16 | 30 | 7 |\n\nHow many bits will be used to encode the letter `b` in the Huffman code for this alphabet?","sol":{"asked":"Depth of b.","idea":"Run Huffman, read b's depth.","method":"Merge 7 and 16 → 23. Next smallest are 21 (b) and 23 → 44, so b becomes a child of the node 44 (one level below it). Then 26 and 30 → 56. Finally 44 and 56 → 100. So b is at depth 2 from the root.","opts":{"A":"1 would mean b is a child of the root. The root children are the nodes 44 and 56.","B":"**Correct.** b is a child of the node 44, which is a child of the root.","C":"3 is the depth of c and e (inside the node 23).","D":"4 is more than any leaf depth in this tree."},"verify":"Code: lengths {'b': 2, 'e': 3, 'c': 3, 'a': 2, 'd': 2}.","trap":"Thinking b is deep because its frequency (21) is not among the largest.","rule":"Depth of the leaf in the finished tree.","transfer":"The codes of a and d have length 2 as well; c and e have length 3.","clue":"The number of bits for a letter is the depth of its leaf.","first":"Merge 7 and 16.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["merge","trees merged","new weight"],"rows":[["1","e (7) + c (16)","23"],["2","b (21) + subtree (23)","44"],["3","a (26) + d (30)","56"],["4","subtree (44) + subtree (56)","100"]],"title":"Merges","stepper":true,"noteLast":false},"fig":{"tree":{"v":"100","l":{"v":"44","l":{"v":"b","s":"21"},"r":{"v":"23","l":{"v":"e","s":"7"},"r":{"v":"c","s":"16"}}},"r":{"v":"56","l":{"v":"a","s":"26"},"r":{"v":"d","s":"30"}}}}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["1","2","3","4"],"key":[1]},{"id":"6406532180383","exam":"14 Aug 2026","qno":"8","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"You are verifying a set of binary codes generated for a text file containing four unique characters: $\\{A, B, C, D\\}$. Which of the following code allocations is invalid because it violates the fundamental prefix property required by Huffman coding?","sol":{"asked":"The set that breaks the prefix property.","idea":"Prefix check.","method":"In option A, `0` (A) is a prefix of `01` (C), and `1` (B) is a prefix of `10` (D). Invalid. The other three have no code that begins another.","opts":{"A":"**Invalid:** A=0 is a prefix of C=01.","B":"Valid prefix code (a chain).","C":"Valid prefix code.","D":"Valid fixed-length code."},"verify":"Code (verify_claims.py): the prefix check reports a violation for option A only.","trap":"Calling D invalid because \"Huffman codes should be different lengths\". Fixed-length codes are prefix-free.","rule":"Prefix property = no code is the start of another.","transfer":"Change C to 11 in option A (A:0, B:1, C:11, D:10): still invalid, because B = 1 is a prefix of both 11 and 10.","clue":"A code set is invalid if some code is the beginning of another.","first":"Look for a one-bit code that starts a two-bit code.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["option","a code that is a prefix of another","valid?"],"rows":[["A","0 is a prefix of 01; 1 is a prefix of 10","**no**"],["B","none","yes"],["C","none","yes"],["D","none (all length 2)","yes"]],"title":"Prefix check","stepper":false,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["$A: 0, B: 1, C: 01, D: 10$","$A: 0, B: 10, C: 110, D: 111$","$A: 1, B: 01, C: 001, D: 000$","$A: 00, B: 01, C: 10, D: 11$"],"key":[0]},{"id":"6406532180385","exam":"14 Aug 2026","qno":"10","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"A text file contains 1000 characters. Originally, each character is represented using a fixed-length code of 4 bits. After applying Huffman coding, the average code length becomes 3 bits per character. What percentage reduction in storage is achieved?","sol":{"asked":"Percent saved.","idea":"$(\\text{old}-\\text{new})/\\text{old}$.","method":"Before $=1000\\cdot4=4000$ bits. After $=1000\\cdot3=3000$ bits. Saved 1000 bits. $1000/4000=25\\%$.","opts":{"A":"20 would be 800 bits saved.","B":"**Correct.** 25 percent.","C":"30 would be 1200 bits saved.","D":"33.33 comes from dividing the saving by the **new** size (1000/3000)."},"verify":"(4-3)/4*100 = 25.0","trap":"Dividing by the new size and getting 33.33.","rule":"Percentage reduction is always relative to the **original**.","transfer":"Original 8 bits, Huffman 5 bits: 37.5 percent.","clue":"Compare total bits before and after.","first":"Before: 4000 bits.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["quantity","bits"],"rows":[["original","4000"],["Huffman","3000"],["saved","1000"],["percent saved","25"]],"title":"Computation","stepper":false,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["20","25","30","33.33"],"key":[1]},{"id":"640653351977","exam":"July 2022","qno":"7","marks":4,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"Which of the following is the correct length of codes for the character set $S = \\{A, B, C, D, E, F\\}$, generated using the Huffman algorithm for given frequencies?\n\n| Character | A | B | C | D | E | F |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Frequency | 13 | 30 | 6 | 35 | 2 | 11 |","sol":{"asked":"The code lengths of all six symbols.","idea":"Huffman tree depths.","method":"Merges: 2+6=8; 8+11=19; 13+19=32; 30+32=62; 35+62=97. The depths: D=1 (child of root), B=2, A=3, F=4, C=5, E=5.","opts":{"A":"B-3 and D-1 do not fit: B is at depth 2.","B":"D-2 is wrong: D (35) is a child of the root, depth 1.","C":"B-3 and D-2 are both wrong.","D":"**Correct.**"},"verify":"Code lengths: {'D': 1, 'B': 2, 'A': 3, 'E': 5, 'C': 5, 'F': 4}. No ties occur in this input, so the lengths are unique.","trap":"Giving B and D the same length because they have similar frequencies. D (35) joins last, directly under the root.","rule":"Heaviest symbol = shortest code; the two lightest (E, C) are the deepest siblings.","transfer":"Weighted total bits: 13*3+30*2+6*5+35*1+2*5+11*4 = 218.","clue":"Run Huffman and read every depth. Then compare with the options.","first":"Merge 2 and 6 first.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["merge","trees merged","new weight"],"rows":[["1","E (2) + C (6)","8"],["2","subtree (8) + F (11)","19"],["3","A (13) + subtree (19)","32"],["4","B (30) + subtree (32)","62"],["5","D (35) + subtree (62)","97"]],"title":"Merges","stepper":true,"noteLast":false},"fig":{"tree":{"v":"97","l":{"v":"D","s":"35"},"r":{"v":"62","l":{"v":"B","s":"30"},"r":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"A","s":"13"},"r":{"v":"19","l":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}},"r":{"v":"F","s":"11"}}}}}}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["A-3, B-3, C-5, D-1, E-5, F-4","A-3, B-2, C-5, D-2, E-5, F-4","A-3, B-3, C-5, D-2, E-5, F-4","A-3, B-2, C-5, D-1, E-5, F-4"],"key":[3]},{"id":"6406531172986","exam":"16 Mar 2025","qno":"11","marks":4,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"Given below is the frequencies for the character set $S = \\{A, B, C, D, E, F\\}$.\n\n| Character | A | B | C | D | E | F |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Frequency | 13 | 30 | 6 | 35 | 2 | 11 |\n\nHuffman encoding is applied on the above character set. Which of the following is the correct length of codes for the character set?","sol":{"asked":"The code lengths of all six symbols.","idea":"Huffman tree depths.","method":"Merges: 2+6=8; 8+11=19; 13+19=32; 30+32=62; 35+62=97. The depths: D=1 (child of root), B=2, A=3, F=4, C=5, E=5.","opts":{"A":"B-3 and D-1 do not fit: B is at depth 2.","B":"D-2 is wrong: D (35) is a child of the root, depth 1.","C":"B-3 and D-2 are both wrong.","D":"**Correct.**"},"verify":"Code lengths: {'D': 1, 'B': 2, 'A': 3, 'E': 5, 'C': 5, 'F': 4}. No ties occur in this input, so the lengths are unique.","trap":"Giving B and D the same length because they have similar frequencies. D (35) joins last, directly under the root.","rule":"Heaviest symbol = shortest code; the two lightest (E, C) are the deepest siblings.","transfer":"Weighted total bits: 13*3+30*2+6*5+35*1+2*5+11*4 = 218.","clue":"Run Huffman and read every depth. Then compare with the options.","first":"Merge 2 and 6 first.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["merge","trees merged","new weight"],"rows":[["1","E (2) + C (6)","8"],["2","subtree (8) + F (11)","19"],["3","A (13) + subtree (19)","32"],["4","B (30) + subtree (32)","62"],["5","D (35) + subtree (62)","97"]],"title":"Merges","stepper":true,"noteLast":false},"fig":{"tree":{"v":"97","l":{"v":"D","s":"35"},"r":{"v":"62","l":{"v":"B","s":"30"},"r":{"v":"32","l":{"v":"A","s":"13"},"r":{"v":"19","l":{"v":"8","l":{"v":"E","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"C","s":"6"}},"r":{"v":"F","s":"11"}}}}}}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["$A-3, B-3, C-5, D-1, E-5, F-4$","$A-3, B-2, C-5, D-2, E-5, F-4$","$A-3, B-3, C-5, D-2, E-5, F-4$","$A-3, B-2, C-5, D-1, E-5, F-4$"],"key":[3]},{"id":"640653690095","exam":"Dec 2023","qno":"7","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"Given the following set of characters and their frequencies:\n\n| Character | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| A | 80 |\n| B | 60 |\n| C | 50 |\n| D | 40 |\n| E | 30 |\n| F | 20 |\n\nUsing Huffman coding, construct the Huffman tree for this set of characters. What is the total number of bits needed to represent the message `ABCDEF`?","sol":{"asked":"Sum of code lengths.","idea":"Huffman lengths, summed.","method":"Merge F(20)+E(30)=50. Now the queue has 40, 50 (C), 50 (the merged node), 60, 80, with a **tie** at 50.\n\n**Tie-break 1 (take the original leaf C first):** 40+50(C)=90; then 50+60=110; then 80+90=170; then 110+170. Depths: A=2, B=2, C=3, D=3, E=3, F=3. Sum = **16**.\n\n**Tie-break 2 (take the merged node first):** 40+50(EF)=90; 50(C)+60=110; 80+90=170. Depths: A=2, B=2, C=2, D=3, E=4, F=4. Sum = **17**.\n\nBoth are valid Huffman trees (both give the same weighted total). The standard implementation (a heap that prefers older entries, i.e. the original leaf) gives 16.","opts":{"A":"15 is below the minimum possible sum (16).","B":"**Correct** (standard tie-break: original leaf first).","C":"17 is the result of the other tie-break. It is also a valid Huffman tree, see the flag.","D":"18 is too large for any Huffman tree on these weights."},"verify":"Code: heap with insertion-order tie-break: lengths sum 16. Explicit alternative tree: 17.","trap":"Forgetting that the question asks for the sum of lengths, not the weighted sum.","rule":"When weights tie, either choice is valid; per-symbol lengths may differ but the weighted total does not.","transfer":"The weighted total is 80*2+60*2+50*3+40*3+30*3+20*3 = 700 for tie-break 1, and 80*2+60*2+50*2+40*3+30*4+20*4 = 700 for tie-break 2.","clue":"\"The message ABCDEF\" has each character once, so the total is the sum of the six code lengths.","first":"Merge 20 and 30 → 50. Now there is a tie at 50.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["tie-break","depths A B C D E F","sum of lengths"],"rows":[["original leaf C first","2 2 3 3 3 3","16"],["merged node first","2 2 2 3 4 4","17"]],"title":"The two tie-breaks","stepper":false,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["15 bits","16 bits","17 bits","18 bits"],"key":[1],"alsoKey":[[2]],"flag":"Tie at weight 50 (C and the merged E+F node). Tie-break \"original leaf first\" gives 16 bits, \"merged node first\" gives 17 bits; both are valid Huffman trees. The site accepts both B and C, with B as the primary key."},{"id":"6406531021266","exam":"01 Dec 2024","qno":"17","marks":4,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"Given the following set of characters and their frequencies:\n\n| Character | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| A | 11 |\n| B | 5 |\n| C | 6 |\n| D | 2 |\n| E | 12 |\n| F | 24 |\n\nUsing Huffman coding, construct the Huffman tree for this set of characters. What is the total number of bits needed to represent the message `ABCDEF`?","sol":{"asked":"Sum of code lengths.","idea":"Huffman lengths, summed.","method":"Merges: 2+5=7; 6+7=13; 11+12=23; 13+23=36; 24+36=60. Depths: F=1, A=3, E=3, C=3, B=4, D=4. Sum $=1+3+3+3+4+4=18$.","opts":{"A":"15 is lower than any valid sum here.","B":"16 is not the sum of these depths.","C":"17 is not the sum of these depths.","D":"**Correct.** 18 bits."},"verify":"Code lengths {'F': 1, 'C': 3, 'D': 4, 'B': 4, 'A': 3, 'E': 3} sum to 18. No ties occur.","trap":"Computing frequency x length, which is the answer to a different question (weighted total bits).","rule":"\"Message ABCDEF\" with one of each → sum of lengths.","transfer":"The weighted total is 11*3+5*4+6*3+2*4+12*3+24*1 = 139.","clue":"Each character once: add the six code lengths.","first":"Merge 2 and 5.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["merge","trees merged","new weight"],"rows":[["1","D (2) + B (5)","7"],["2","C (6) + subtree (7)","13"],["3","A (11) + E (12)","23"],["4","subtree (13) + subtree (23)","36"],["5","F (24) + subtree (36)","60"]],"title":"Merges","stepper":true,"noteLast":false},"fig":{"tree":{"v":"60","l":{"v":"F","s":"24"},"r":{"v":"36","l":{"v":"13","l":{"v":"C","s":"6"},"r":{"v":"7","l":{"v":"D","s":"2"},"r":{"v":"B","s":"5"}}},"r":{"v":"23","l":{"v":"A","s":"11"},"r":{"v":"E","s":"12"}}}}}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["15 bits","16 bits","17 bits","18 bits"],"key":[3]},{"id":"640653771120","exam":"24 Mar 2024","qno":"10","marks":2,"type":"MSQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"For a set of symbols with probabilities of occurrence, which of the following statement(s) about the Huffman tree is/are correct?","sol":{"asked":"All correct statements.","idea":"Properties of a Huffman tree.","method":"Higher probability symbols are merged late, so they sit near the root (A true). Lower probability symbols are merged early and sit deep (B false). The tree is strict binary but not complete (C false): in the earlier example depths ranged from 1 to 5. Symbols are leaves, so codes are prefix-free (D true).","opts":{"A":"**True.**","B":"False: the rare symbols are deepest.","C":"False: it is strict (full), not complete.","D":"**True.**"},"verify":"Checked on the 6-symbol example: depths 1 to 5, not complete.","trap":"Confusing \"strict binary\" with \"complete binary\".","rule":"Frequent = shallow, rare = deep, leaves = prefix-free.","transfer":"Is the tree always balanced? No.","clue":"Think of how the tree is built: the lightest symbols are merged first.","first":"Lightest symbols end up deepest."},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["Symbols with higher probabilities are generally closer to the root of the tree","Symbols with lower probabilities are generally closer to the root of the tree","The Huffman tree is always a complete binary tree","It generates prefix codes (no code is a prefix of another)."],"key":[0,3]},{"id":"640653854180","exam":"04 Aug 2024","qno":"16","marks":3,"type":"MSQ","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"For a set of symbols with probabilities of occurrence, which of the following statement(s) is/are true about the Huffman algorithm?","sol":{"asked":"All true statements.","idea":"Properties of the Huffman tree.","method":"A false: low probability means deep. B true: every merge creates a node with exactly two children. C true: symbols are leaves. D true: with distinct probabilities the two smallest are merged first, so they are siblings at the same depth. E false: four equal frequencies give a perfect tree where every code has length 2.","opts":{"A":"False.","B":"**True.**","C":"**True.**","D":"**True.**","E":"False: counter-example with four equal frequencies."},"verify":"Code: huffman({a:1,b:1,c:1,d:1}) gives all lengths 2.","trap":"Option E sounds natural but fails for equal frequencies.","rule":"Use small counter-examples to kill \"always\" statements.","transfer":"With 2 symbols, both codes have length 1.","clue":"Five statements: test each on the build rule and on small examples.","first":"Lowest probabilities are merged first, so they are siblings.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["statement","verdict","reason / example"],"rows":[["A","false","rare symbols are deepest"],["B","true","each merge adds two children"],["C","true","leaves"],["D","true","merged first, so siblings"],["E","false","frequencies 1,1,1,1: all codes length 2"]],"title":"Verdicts","stepper":false,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["Symbols with lower probabilities value are generally closer to the root of the Huffman tree.","The Huffman tree is always a strict binary tree (each node has two children, except leaf nodes).","It generates prefix codes (no code is a prefix of another).","If probability values are distinct, then two symbols with the lowest probabilities always have the same code length.","There is always at least one symbol with a code of length one."],"key":[1,2,3]},{"id":"640653586789","exam":"Aug 2023","qno":"12","marks":3,"type":"SA","idea":"Huffman Coding","sec":"huff","stem":"An entire message is created using characters from the set $S = \\{A, B, C, D, E\\}$. The probability of occurrence of each character is given in the table below.\n\n| A | B | C | D | E |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 0.22 | 0.21 | 0.16 | 0.30 | 0.11 |\n\nHow many bits will be used to encode the message `ABCDE` using Huffman codes?","sol":{"asked":"Sum of code lengths.","idea":"Huffman lengths, summed.","method":"Merges: 0.11+0.16=0.27; 0.21+0.22=0.43; 0.27+0.30=0.57; 0.43+0.57=1.00. Depths: A=2, B=2, D=2, C=3, E=3. Sum = 12.","verify":"Code: lengths {'B': 2, 'A': 2, 'E': 3, 'C': 3, 'D': 2}, sum 12.","trap":"Multiplying by probabilities (average bits) instead of adding the lengths.","rule":"One of each symbol → sum of lengths.","transfer":"The average code length (weighted) is 0.22*2+0.21*2+0.16*3+0.30*2+0.11*3 = 2.27 bits per symbol.","clue":"The message has each character once, so add the five code lengths.","first":"Merge 0.11 and 0.16.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["merge","trees merged","new weight"],"rows":[["1","E (0.11) + C (0.16)","0.27"],["2","B (0.21) + A (0.22)","0.43"],["3","0.27 + D (0.30)","0.57"],["4","0.43 + 0.57","1.00"]],"title":"Merges","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"SRC","num":{"ans":12,"tol":0}},{"id":"6406532180384","exam":"14 Aug 2026","qno":"9","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"Consider the following activities represented as `(Start Time, Finish Time)`:\n$A1 = (8, 11)$\n$A2 = (1, 4)$\n$A3 = (3, 5)$\n$A4 = (0, 6)$\n$A5 = (5, 7)$\n$A6 = (3, 9)$\n$A7 = (5, 9)$\n$A8 = (6, 10)$\n$A9 = (8, 12)$\n$A10 = (12, 14)$\n\nTwo activities are considered compatible if the start time of one activity is greater than or equal to the finish time of the previously selected activity. A greedy algorithm is used to select the maximum number of non-overlapping activities. How many activities will be selected?","sol":{"asked":"Number of activities the greedy algorithm selects.","idea":"Earliest-finish-first greedy.","method":"Sorted by finish time, scan and take an activity when its start $\\ge$ the finish of the last chosen. The table shows each decision. Chosen: A2, A5, A1, A10, so the maximum is **4**.","opts":{"A":"3 misses one compatible activity.","B":"**Correct.** A2, A5, A1, A10.","C":"5 would need an extra compatible activity; none exists.","D":"6 is more than the maximum."},"verify":"Code: max_activities returns 4 activities.","trap":"Sorting by start time or by duration. Only earliest finish is optimal.","rule":"Max number of non-overlapping activities → sort by finish, scan.","transfer":"Removing an activity that was **not** chosen never changes the maximum.","clue":"Sort by finish time and take every activity that starts at or after the last chosen finish.","first":"Find the earliest finishing activity.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["activity","start","finish","last chosen finish","decision"],"rows":[["A2","1","4","-","take"],["A3","3","5","4","skip"],["A4","0","6","4","skip"],["A5","5","7","4","take"],["A6","3","9","7","skip"],["A7","5","9","7","skip"],["A8","6","10","7","skip"],["A1","8","11","7","take"],["A9","8","12","11","skip"],["A10","12","14","11","take"]],"title":"Earliest-finish scan","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["3","4","5","6"],"key":[1],"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"A1","s":8,"e":11},{"name":"A2","s":1,"e":4},{"name":"A3","s":3,"e":5},{"name":"A4","s":0,"e":6},{"name":"A5","s":5,"e":7},{"name":"A6","s":3,"e":9},{"name":"A7","s":5,"e":9},{"name":"A8","s":6,"e":10},{"name":"A9","s":8,"e":12},{"name":"A10","s":12,"e":14}],"title":""},"caption":"The activities on a time line (redrawn from the table)."}},{"id":"640653586790","exam":"Aug 2023","qno":"13","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"Meetings M1, M2, ....., M10 are to be conducted in a single available meeting room. The table below gives the start and end times of these meetings. If any activity finishes at time $T$, then other activities can be started at time $T$ or afterward.\n\n| | M1 | M2 | M3 | M4 | M5 | M6 | M7 | M8 | M9 | M10 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| start | 3 | 2 | 8 | 4 | 10 | 1 | 12 | 3 | 1 | 6 |\n| end | 6 | 3 | 11 | 6 | 11 | 2 | 13 | 5 | 3 | 9 |\n\nHow many meetings can be scheduled at most by following the timing constraints given above?","sol":{"asked":"Maximum number of meetings in the single room.","idea":"Earliest-finish-first greedy.","method":"Sorted by finish time, scan and take an activity when its start $\\ge$ the finish of the last chosen. The table shows each decision. Chosen: M6, M2, M8, M10, M5, M7, so the maximum is **6**.","opts":{"A":"4 misses compatible meetings.","B":"5 misses one.","C":"**Correct.** 6 meetings.","D":"7 is more than the maximum."},"verify":"Code: max_activities returns 6 activities.","trap":"Sorting by start time or by duration. Only earliest finish is optimal.","rule":"Max number of non-overlapping activities → sort by finish, scan.","transfer":"Removing an activity that was **not** chosen never changes the maximum.","clue":"Sort by finish time and take every activity that starts at or after the last chosen finish.","first":"Find the earliest finishing activity.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["activity","start","finish","last chosen finish","decision"],"rows":[["M6","1","2","-","take"],["M9","1","3","2","skip"],["M2","2","3","2","take"],["M8","3","5","3","take"],["M1","3","6","5","skip"],["M4","4","6","5","skip"],["M10","6","9","5","take"],["M3","8","11","9","skip"],["M5","10","11","9","take"],["M7","12","13","11","take"]],"title":"Earliest-finish scan","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["4","5","6","7"],"key":[2],"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":3,"e":6},{"name":"M2","s":2,"e":3},{"name":"M3","s":8,"e":11},{"name":"M4","s":4,"e":6},{"name":"M5","s":10,"e":11},{"name":"M6","s":1,"e":2},{"name":"M7","s":12,"e":13},{"name":"M8","s":3,"e":5},{"name":"M9","s":1,"e":3},{"name":"M10","s":6,"e":9}],"title":""},"caption":"The activities on a time line (redrawn from the table)."}},{"id":"640653690096","exam":"Dec 2023","qno":"8","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"Suppose you have a set of jobs with their start and end times. If any job finishes at a time $T$, then the other job can be scheduled at a time $T$ or afterward. Your goal is to find the maximum number of non-overlapping jobs that can be scheduled.\nWhich of the following greedy strategy can be used to solve this problem optimally?","sol":{"asked":"The optimal greedy strategy.","idea":"Earliest finish first.","method":"Selecting the job that ends earliest leaves the largest remaining time window. The other rules each have a counter-example (see the table below).","opts":{"A":"Fails: one early long job blocks everything.","B":"**Correct.**","C":"Fails: a short job in the middle can block two others.","D":"Fails: long jobs block many."},"verify":"Brute force on the counter-examples confirms the table.","trap":"Choosing \"shortest duration\" because short jobs \"use less room\".","rule":"Max number of activities → earliest finish.","transfer":"For minimum rooms the rule is different: sweep for the busiest instant.","clue":"Which rule leaves the most room for the remaining jobs?","first":"Earliest finish.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["rule","counter-example","result"],"rows":[["earliest start","(0,10), (1,2), (3,4), (5,6)","picks 1, optimum 3"],["shortest duration","(1,6), (5,8), (7,12)","picks (5,8) alone: 1; optimum 2"],["longest duration","(0,9), (1,2), (3,4)","picks 1; optimum 2"],["**earliest end**","proved by exchange","**optimal**"]],"title":"Why the other rules fail","stepper":false,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["Select jobs with the earliest start times first.","Select jobs with the earliest end times first.","Select jobs with the shortest duration first.","Select jobs with the longest duration first."],"key":[1]},{"id":"6406531172991","exam":"16 Mar 2025","qno":"9","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"You are given the following list of 8 meeting requests with start time, and end time.\n\n| Meeting Id | Start Time | End Time |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 1 | 4 |\n| 2 | 1 | 2 |\n| 3 | 5 | 8 |\n| 4 | 7 | 10 |\n| 5 | 6 | 8 |\n| 6 | 11 | 16 |\n| 7 | 15 | 20 |\n| 8 | 14 | 17 |\n\nEach meeting requires its own conference room. Your goal is to schedule all meetings in the minimum number of conference rooms. Assume that if any meeting ends at time $t$, then another meeting can start at time $t$ or afterwards in the same room.\nThe minimum number of conference rooms required to schedule all meetings is ___","sol":{"asked":"The peak number of simultaneous meetings.","idea":"Sweep line: +1 at start, -1 at end, ends first.","method":"Meetings 3, 4, 5 overlap on (7, 8): that is 3 at once. Meetings 6, 7, 8 overlap on (15, 16): 3 at once. Meetings 1 and 2 overlap on (1, 2): 2. No instant has 4, so **3** rooms are enough and needed.","opts":{"A":"**Correct.** Peak of 3 at times 7 and 15.","B":"4 would need four simultaneous meetings; none exist.","C":"5 is more than the number of meetings in any group.","D":"6 would mean no two meetings share a room."},"verify":"Code: rooms() = 3.","trap":"Giving 4 or 5 by counting overlapping pairs rather than simultaneous meetings.","rule":"Rooms = peak simultaneous meetings.","transfer":"Change M4 to (11,12): the first group peaks at 2, the second at 3, answer still 3.","clue":"Minimum rooms = the largest number of meetings running at the same time.","first":"Look for overlapping groups: (5,8), (6,8), (7,10) and (14,17), (15,20), (11,16).","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["time","event","running"],"rows":[["1","+M1, +M2","2"],["2","-M2","1"],["4","-M1","0"],["5","+M3","1"],["6","+M5","2"],["7","+M4","3"],["8","-M3, -M5","1"],["10","-M4","0"],["11","+M6","1"],["14","+M8","2"],["15","+M7","3"],["16","-M6","2"],["17","-M8","1"],["20","-M7","0"]],"title":"Sweep: peak 3","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["3","4","5","6"],"key":[0],"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"1","s":1,"e":4},{"name":"2","s":1,"e":2},{"name":"3","s":5,"e":8},{"name":"4","s":7,"e":10},{"name":"5","s":6,"e":8},{"name":"6","s":11,"e":16},{"name":"7","s":15,"e":20},{"name":"8","s":14,"e":17}],"title":""},"caption":"The activities on a time line (redrawn from the table)."}},{"id":"6406532035103","exam":"06 Apr 2026","qno":"14","marks":4,"type":"SA","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"In the table below, we have 9 activities with the corresponding start and finish times. It might not be possible to complete all the activities since their time frames can conflict. For example, if any activity starts at time 0 and finishes at time 4, then other activities can not begin before 4. It can be started at 4 or later.\nWhat is the maximum number of activities which can be performed without conflict?\n\n| Activity | Start time | Finish time |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A | 1 | 4 |\n| B | 3 | 4 |\n| C | 10 | 11 |\n| D | 2 | 3 |\n| E | 4 | 5 |\n| F | 7 | 9 |\n| G | 11 | 14 |\n| H | 6 | 8 |\n| I | 5 | 9 |","sol":{"asked":"The maximum number of compatible activities.","idea":"Earliest-finish-first greedy.","method":"Sorted by finish time, scan and take an activity when its start $\\ge$ the finish of the last chosen. The table shows each decision. Chosen: D, B, E, H, C, G, so the maximum is **6**.","verify":"Code: max_activities returns 6 activities.","trap":"Sorting by start time or by duration. Only earliest finish is optimal.","rule":"Max number of non-overlapping activities → sort by finish, scan.","transfer":"Removing an activity that was **not** chosen never changes the maximum.","clue":"Sort by finish time and take every activity that starts at or after the last chosen finish.","first":"Find the earliest finishing activity.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["activity","start","finish","last chosen finish","decision"],"rows":[["D","2","3","-","take"],["A","1","4","3","skip"],["B","3","4","3","take"],["E","4","5","4","take"],["H","6","8","5","take"],["I","5","9","8","skip"],["F","7","9","8","skip"],["C","10","11","8","take"],["G","11","14","11","take"]],"title":"Earliest-finish scan","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"SRC","num":{"ans":6,"tol":0},"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"A","s":1,"e":4},{"name":"B","s":3,"e":4},{"name":"C","s":10,"e":11},{"name":"D","s":2,"e":3},{"name":"E","s":4,"e":5},{"name":"F","s":7,"e":9},{"name":"G","s":11,"e":14},{"name":"H","s":6,"e":8},{"name":"I","s":5,"e":9}],"title":""},"caption":"The activities on a time line (redrawn from the table)."}},{"id":"640653351978","exam":"July 2022","qno":"13","marks":4,"type":"SA","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"Meetings M1, M2, ....., M11 are to be conducted in a single available meeting room. The table below gives the start and end times of these meetings. If any activity finishes at time $T$, then other activities can be started at time $T$ or afterward. What is the maximum number of meetings that can be held in the meeting room without conflicts?\n\n| | M1 | M2 | M3 | M4 | M5 | M6 | M7 | M8 | M9 | M10 | M11 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Start | 5 | 4 | 8 | 9 | 12 | 7 | 5 | 3 | 4 | 1 | 8 |\n| End | 7 | 5 | 10 | 11 | 14 | 9 | 8 | 5 | 9 | 3 | 13 |","sol":{"asked":"The maximum number of meetings.","idea":"Earliest-finish-first greedy.","method":"Sorted by finish time, scan and take an activity when its start $\\ge$ the finish of the last chosen. The table shows each decision. Chosen: M10, M8, M1, M6, M4, M5, so the maximum is **6**.","verify":"Code: max_activities returns 6 activities.","trap":"Sorting by start time or by duration. Only earliest finish is optimal.","rule":"Max number of non-overlapping activities → sort by finish, scan.","transfer":"Removing an activity that was **not** chosen never changes the maximum.","clue":"Sort by finish time and take every activity that starts at or after the last chosen finish.","first":"Find the earliest finishing activity.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["activity","start","finish","last chosen finish","decision"],"rows":[["M10","1","3","-","take"],["M8","3","5","3","take"],["M2","4","5","5","skip"],["M1","5","7","5","take"],["M7","5","8","7","skip"],["M9","4","9","7","skip"],["M6","7","9","7","take"],["M3","8","10","9","skip"],["M4","9","11","9","take"],["M11","8","13","11","skip"],["M5","12","14","11","take"]],"title":"Earliest-finish scan","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"SRC","num":{"ans":6,"tol":0},"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":5,"e":7},{"name":"M2","s":4,"e":5},{"name":"M3","s":8,"e":10},{"name":"M4","s":9,"e":11},{"name":"M5","s":12,"e":14},{"name":"M6","s":7,"e":9},{"name":"M7","s":5,"e":8},{"name":"M8","s":3,"e":5},{"name":"M9","s":4,"e":9},{"name":"M10","s":1,"e":3},{"name":"M11","s":8,"e":13}],"title":""},"caption":"The activities on a time line (redrawn from the table)."}},{"id":"640653771121","exam":"24 Mar 2024","qno":"16","marks":4,"type":"SA","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"You are given a list of 7 activities to be conducted in a single available room, each represented by `(start time, end time)`. If any activity finishes at time $T$, then another activity can be started at time $T$ or afterwards.\nActivities: `[(1, 4), (3, 5), (0, 2), (2, 3), (5, 8), (8, 9), (5, 7)]`\nHow many activities can be scheduled at most by following the timing constraints given above?","sol":{"asked":"The maximum number of activities.","idea":"Earliest-finish-first greedy.","method":"Sorted by finish time, scan and take an activity when its start $\\ge$ the finish of the last chosen. The table shows each decision. Chosen: A3, A4, A2, A7, A6, so the maximum is **5**.","verify":"Code: max_activities returns 5 activities.","trap":"Sorting by start time or by duration. Only earliest finish is optimal.","rule":"Max number of non-overlapping activities → sort by finish, scan.","transfer":"Removing an activity that was **not** chosen never changes the maximum.","clue":"Sort by finish time and take every activity that starts at or after the last chosen finish.","first":"Find the earliest finishing activity.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["activity","start","finish","last chosen finish","decision"],"rows":[["A3","0","2","-","take"],["A4","2","3","2","take"],["A1","1","4","3","skip"],["A2","3","5","3","take"],["A7","5","7","5","take"],["A5","5","8","7","skip"],["A6","8","9","7","take"]],"title":"Earliest-finish scan","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"SRC","num":{"ans":5,"tol":0},"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"A1","s":1,"e":4},{"name":"A2","s":3,"e":5},{"name":"A3","s":0,"e":2},{"name":"A4","s":2,"e":3},{"name":"A5","s":5,"e":8},{"name":"A6","s":8,"e":9},{"name":"A7","s":5,"e":7}],"title":""},"caption":"The activities on a time line (redrawn from the table)."}},{"id":"640653771122","exam":"24 Mar 2024","qno":"17","marks":4,"type":"SA","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"You are given a list of 8 meetings, each represented by a tuple `(start time, end time)`. Your goal is to schedule all meetings in the minimum number of conference rooms. If a meeting ends at time $t$ in a conference room, another meeting can start at time $t$ or afterwards in the same room.\nMeetings: `[(1, 4), (6, 12), (2, 8), (11, 15), (3, 7), (5, 10), (9, 14), (13, 16)]`\nThe minimum number of conference rooms needed to schedule all meetings is ___.","sol":{"asked":"Minimum rooms.","idea":"Sweep line / min-heap of room end times.","method":"At time 6 the meetings (2,8), (3,7), (5,10) and (6,12) are all in progress: **4** at once. Check no instant has 5. So 4 rooms are needed.","verify":"Code: rooms() = 4.","trap":"Answering 3 by looking at the first three meetings only.","rule":"Rooms = peak overlap.","transfer":"Remove (5,10): the peak becomes 3.","clue":"Peak number of meetings running at the same instant.","first":"Sweep over start and end events.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["time","event","running"],"rows":[["1","+(1,4)","1"],["2","+(2,8)","2"],["3","+(3,7)","3"],["4","-(1,4)","2"],["5","+(5,10)","3"],["6","+(6,12)","4"],["7","-(3,7)","3"],["8","-(2,8)","2"],["9","+(9,14)","3"],["10","-(5,10)","2"],["11","+(11,15)","3"],["12","-(6,12)","2"],["13","+(13,16)","3"],["14","-(9,14)","2"],["15","-(11,15)","1"],["16","-(13,16)","0"]],"title":"Sweep: peak 4 at time 6","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"SRC","num":{"ans":4,"tol":0},"fig":{"timeline":{"items":[{"name":"M1","s":1,"e":4},{"name":"M2","s":6,"e":12},{"name":"M3","s":2,"e":8},{"name":"M4","s":11,"e":15},{"name":"M5","s":3,"e":7},{"name":"M6","s":5,"e":10},{"name":"M7","s":9,"e":14},{"name":"M8","s":13,"e":16}],"title":""},"caption":"The activities on a time line (redrawn from the table)."}},{"id":"6406531021264","exam":"01 Dec 2024","qno":"12","marks":3,"type":"SA","idea":"Interval Scheduling & Meeting Rooms","sec":"ivl","stem":"For an activity $X$, $X_s$ is the starting time and $X_e$ is the ending time. The following are the starting time and ending time of activities A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H and I given in chronological order.\n$A_s, B_s, C_s, A_e, C_e, D_s, E_s, B_e, F_s, G_s, H_s, E_e, I_s, D_e, F_e, G_e, H_e, I_e$\nWhat is the minimum number of rooms required to schedule the activities in a set of rooms such that there are no conflicts?","sol":{"asked":"The maximum running count.","idea":"Sweep line on the given event order.","method":"Walk the events in order, adding 1 for each start and subtracting 1 for each end. The running count first reaches **5** at the event $H_s$ (position 11 in the table) and never exceeds 5.","verify":"Code: max running count = 5.","trap":"Counting the starts (9 activities) instead of the peak.","rule":"Events already ordered → just keep the running total.","transfer":"Moving $A_e$ to the very end would raise the peak by one.","clue":"The list is already in time order. Keep a running count: start = +1, end = -1.","first":"A, B, C all start before any ends.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["position","event","running"],"rows":[["1","As","1"],["2","Bs","2"],["3","Cs","3"],["4","Ae","2"],["5","Ce","1"],["6","Ds","2"],["7","Es","3"],["8","Be","2"],["9","Fs","3"],["10","Gs","4"],["11","Hs","5"],["12","Ee","4"],["13","Is","5"],["14","De","4"],["15","Fe","3"],["16","Ge","2"],["17","He","1"],["18","Ie","0"]],"title":"Running count","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"SRC","num":{"ans":5,"tol":0}},{"id":"6406532035105","exam":"06 Apr 2026","qno":"7","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Minimizing Maximum Lateness","sec":"late","stem":"Consider a set of activities $A_1, A_2, \\dots, A_n$, where each activity $A_i$ has: a processing time $T_i$ (time required to complete the activity), and a deadline $D_i$. Only one activity can be executed at a time. If an activity finishes at time $t$, the next activity can start at time $t$ itself. Assume that the initial start time is 0. The lateness of an activity $A_i$ is defined as:\n\n$$L(i) = \\begin{cases} F_i - D_i, & \\text{if } F_i > D_i \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases}$$\n\nwhere $F_i$ denotes the finish time of activity $A_i$. Let $M_L$ denote the maximum lateness among all activities: $M_L = \\max(L_1, L_2, \\dots, L_n)$.\nThe objective is to schedule the activities in an order that minimizes $M_L$. Which of the following greedy strategies will always produce an optimal schedule for minimizing the maximum lateness $M_L$?","sol":{"asked":"The strategy that is always optimal.","idea":"EDF with the exchange argument.","method":"Earliest deadline first is the standard optimal rule. The others have counter-examples:\n- Shortest first: jobs (T=1,D=100) and (T=2,D=2): shortest-first makes the urgent job finish at 3, lateness 1; EDF has maximum lateness 0.\n- Smallest slack: jobs X(T=2,D=10), Y(T=9,D=11): slack-first runs Y first, X finishes at 11, lateness 1; EDF has 0.\n- Longest first: jobs (T=5,D=20) and (T=1,D=1): longest-first finishes the short urgent job at 6, lateness 5; EDF has lateness 0.","opts":{"A":"Fails: shortest-first can make an urgent job late (see the counter-example (1,100) and (2,2)).","B":"**Correct.** Earliest deadline first.","C":"Fails: the slack rule is not optimal (counter-example X, Y above).","D":"Fails: longest first can delay urgent short jobs."},"verify":"Code (verify_claims.py): all three counter-examples give a positive lateness for the wrong rule and 0 for EDF.","trap":"Choosing slack ($D_i-T_i$) because it \"accounts for both numbers\".","rule":"Minimizing maximum lateness → earliest deadline first.","transfer":"If the objective were the **number** of late jobs, the rule would be different (Moore-Hodgson), not asked here.","clue":"The proven rule is the one tied to deadlines only.","first":"Earliest deadline first."},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["Always schedule first the activity with the minimum processing time.","Always schedule first the activity with the earliest deadline.","Always schedule first the activity with the minimum value of $D_i - T_i$.","Always schedule first the activity with the maximum processing time."],"key":[1]},{"id":"640653854181","exam":"04 Aug 2024","qno":"10","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Minimizing Maximum Lateness","sec":"late","stem":"Consider the following activities $A_1, A_2, \\dots, A_6$.\n\n| Task | $A_1$ | $A_2$ | $A_3$ | $A_4$ | $A_5$ | $A_6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Activity Duration($T$) | 4 | 2 | 1 | 4 | 3 | 2 |\n| Deadline($D$) | 6 | 7 | 8 | 12 | 14 | 5 |\n\nWe can execute one activity at a time. Each activity $A_i$ has activity duration $T_i$ which represents the time required to complete and a deadline $D_i$. Consider the initial start time 0 to start the first activity.\nThe lateness of each job $i$ is defined as follows:\n\n$$L(i) = \\begin{cases} \\text{Finish Time}(F_i) - \\text{Deadline}(D_i) & \\text{if } \\text{Finish Time}(F_i) > \\text{Deadline}(D_i) \\\\ 0 & \\text{else} \\end{cases}$$\n\nSuppose $M_L$ is the maximum lateness of all activities lateness:-\n$M_L = \\text{Max}(L_1, L_2, \\dots, L_n)$\nWhat will be minimum possible value of $M_L$?","sol":{"asked":"The minimum possible maximum lateness.","idea":"EDF gives the optimum.","method":"Run the jobs in deadline order. Finish times are running sums. Lateness is $\\max(0,F-D)$. The largest lateness is **2**, and since EDF is optimal this is the minimum.","opts":{"A":"4 is a valid but not minimal value.","B":"3 is larger than the minimum.","C":"**Correct.** 2.","D":"1 is not reachable: the best order still has a job that is 2 late."},"verify":"Code: EDF 2, brute force over all 720 orders also 2.","trap":"Using the order of the table (A1, A2, ...) instead of sorting by deadline.","rule":"Min max lateness = EDF.","transfer":"Add a job with a very late deadline last: the maximum does not change.","clue":"Sort by deadline, run in that order, and add the durations to get finish times.","first":"Order the jobs by deadline.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["job","$T$","$D$","finish $F$","lateness $L$"],"rows":[["A6","2","5","2","0"],["A1","4","6","6","0"],["A2","2","7","8","1"],["A3","1","8","9","1"],["A4","4","12","13","1"],["A5","3","14","16","2"]],"title":"EDF schedule","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["4","3","2","1"],"key":[2]},{"id":"6406531172987","exam":"16 Mar 2025","qno":"5","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"Minimizing Maximum Lateness","sec":"late","stem":"Consider the following activities $A_1, A_2, \\dots, A_7$.\n\n| Task | $A_1$ | $A_2$ | $A_3$ | $A_4$ | $A_5$ | $A_6$ | $A_7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Activity Duration($T$) | 4 | 2 | 1 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 5 |\n| Deadline($D$) | 14 | 4 | 9 | 7 | 19 | 2 | 12 |\n\nWe can execute one activity at a time. Each activity $A_i$ has activity duration $T_i$ which represents the time required to complete and a deadline $D_i$. Consider the initial start time 0 to start the first selected activity.\nLet the lateness of a schedule be the maximum amount by which any single activity is late in that schedule.\nWhat is the minimum lateness across all possible schedules?","sol":{"asked":"The minimum possible maximum lateness.","idea":"EDF gives the optimum.","method":"Run the jobs in deadline order. Finish times are running sums. Lateness is $\\max(0,F-D)$. The largest lateness is **4**, and since EDF is optimal this is the minimum.","opts":{"A":"2 is below the minimum.","B":"3 is below the minimum.","C":"**Correct.** 4.","D":"5 is not the minimum."},"verify":"Code: EDF 4, brute force over all 5040 orders also 4.","trap":"Using the order of the table (A1, A2, ...) instead of sorting by deadline.","rule":"Min max lateness = EDF.","transfer":"Add a job with a very late deadline last: the maximum does not change.","clue":"Sort by deadline, run in that order, and add the durations to get finish times.","first":"Order the jobs by deadline.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["job","$T$","$D$","finish $F$","lateness $L$"],"rows":[["A6","2","2","2","0"],["A2","2","4","4","0"],["A4","4","7","8","1"],["A3","1","9","9","0"],["A7","5","12","14","2"],["A1","4","14","18","4"],["A5","3","19","21","2"]],"title":"EDF schedule","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["2","3","4","5"],"key":[2]},{"id":"6406531338313","exam":"03 Aug 2025","qno":"7","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"General Greedy Concepts & Counter-Examples","sec":"idea","stem":"Consider the following statements and select the correct option.\ni. The greedy technique always gives an optimized solution to the problem.\nii. Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshal algorithms use the greedy technique to solve the path problems.","sol":{"asked":"Truth values of (i) and (ii).","idea":"Greedy is not always optimal; Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall are dynamic programming.","method":"(i) False: coins {1,3,4}, pay 6 gives greedy 4+1+1 = 3 coins while 3+3 = 2 coins. (ii) False: Bellman-Ford relaxes all edges repeatedly and Floyd-Warshall fills a table over intermediate vertices: both are dynamic programming, not greedy (Dijkstra and Prim are the greedy ones).","opts":{"A":"**Correct.** Both false.","B":"Both true is impossible: (i) has a counter-example.","C":"(i) is false.","D":"(ii) is false: those are dynamic programming."},"verify":"Code (verify_claims.py): greedy uses 3 coins, dynamic programming finds 2.","trap":"Believing every named shortest-path algorithm is greedy.","rule":"Greedy: Dijkstra, Prim, Kruskal, Huffman. DP: Bellman-Ford, Floyd-Warshall.","transfer":"Is Dijkstra greedy? Yes.","clue":"Judge each statement separately.","first":"Think of a counter-example for (i)."},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["Statements (i) and (ii) are false.","Statements (i) and (ii) are true.","Statement (i) is true and Statement (ii) is false.","Statement (i) is false and Statement (ii) is true."],"key":[0]},{"id":"6406531021265","exam":"01 Dec 2024","qno":"6","marks":3,"type":"MCQ","idea":"General Greedy Concepts & Counter-Examples","sec":"idea","stem":"Let you have a single positive integer $x$, which is initially equal to 1. You are given a positive integer $n$ where $n > x$.\nIn each step, you can either increment $x$ by 1 or double $x$. Your goal is to produce a target value $n$ in minimum number of steps.\nFor example, you can produce the integer $n = 10$ in minimum four steps as follows:\n$1(+1) \\to 2(*2) \\to 4(+1) \\to 5(*2) \\to 10$\nWhat is the minimum number of steps required if $n = 725$?","sol":{"asked":"Fewest steps to reach 725.","idea":"Backward greedy: halving is always better than subtracting when the number is even.","method":"Reverse the process: from $n$, if even, halve (undoing a doubling); if odd, subtract 1 (undoing +1). Count the moves until you reach 1.","opts":{"A":"**Correct.** 14 steps.","B":"15 is one too many.","C":"16 is two too many.","D":"17 is too many: a breadth-first search over all moves also gives 14."},"verify":"Code (verify_claims.py): breadth-first search over all values from 1 to 725 gives 14 (and 4 for n = 10).","trap":"Going forward and always doubling: you overshoot. Backward, an even number is always halved.","rule":"Greedy works backwards here: halve if even, subtract 1 if odd.","transfer":"For n = 10 the same rule gives 4 steps, matching the example in the question.","clue":"Work backwards from 725: halve when even, subtract 1 when odd.","first":"725 is odd, so the last move was +1.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["value","even or odd","reverse move","moves so far"],"rows":[["725","odd","-1 → 724","1"],["724","even","/2 → 362","2"],["362","even","/2 → 181","3"],["181","odd","-1 → 180","4"],["180","even","/2 → 90","5"],["90","even","/2 → 45","6"],["45","odd","-1 → 44","7"],["44","even","/2 → 22","8"],["22","even","/2 → 11","9"],["11","odd","-1 → 10","10"],["10","even","/2 → 5","11"],["5","odd","-1 → 4","12"],["4","even","/2 → 2","13"],["2","even","/2 → 1","14"]],"title":"Backward greedy from 725","stepper":true,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["14","15","16","17"],"key":[0]},{"id":"640653854182","exam":"04 Aug 2024","qno":"17","marks":3,"type":"MSQ","idea":"General Greedy Concepts & Counter-Examples","sec":"idea","stem":"Let `coins` be a list of $k$ coin denominations (positive integers) that are available in as much quantity as you want. You want to pay a sum of Rupees $x$ using as few coins as possible from the coin denominations given in the list `coins`.\nConsider the following greedy strategy: Sort the coin denominations in descending order, and then repeatedly choose the largest coin denomination that is less than or equal to the remaining amount $x$. We subtract the chosen coin value from $x$ and continue this process until $x$ becomes zero.\nWhich of the following is/are counter-example(s) that prove that a given greedy strategy is not optimum?","sol":{"asked":"All options where greedy is worse than optimal.","idea":"Compare greedy with the true minimum (dynamic programming or inspection).","method":"**A:** greedy takes 10, 2, 1 = 3 coins, but 8+5 = 2 coins. Counter-example. **B:** greedy 10, 10, 5 = 3; optimum 3. **C:** greedy 7, 7, 1 = 3; optimum 3 (5+5+5). **D:** greedy 15, 15, 1×5 = 7; optimum 15+10+10 = 3. Counter-example. **E:** greedy 25, 10, 5 = 3; optimum 3.","opts":{"A":"**Counter-example:** 3 coins vs 2.","B":"Greedy 3 = optimal 3.","C":"Greedy 3 = optimal 3.","D":"**Counter-example:** 7 coins vs 3.","E":"Greedy 3 = optimal 3."},"verify":"Dynamic programming minimum computed for all five; only A and D differ.","trap":"Choosing an option where greedy happens to match the optimum. The counter-example must show a **worse** greedy answer.","rule":"To disprove greedy: one input where greedy uses more coins.","transfer":"Add coin 12 to option B: x=25 greedy 12+12+1 = 3, still 3.","clue":"A counter-example needs greedy to use **more** coins than the optimum.","first":"For each option compute the greedy count and the best count.","trace":{"t":"trace","head":["option","coins","amount","greedy","optimal","counter-example?"],"rows":[["A","[1, 2, 5, 8, 9, 10]","13","3","2","**yes**"],["B","[1, 2, 5, 10]","25","3","3","no"],["C","[1, 5, 6, 7]","15","3","3","no"],["D","[1, 6, 7, 10, 15]","35","7","3","**yes**"],["E","[1, 2, 5, 10, 25]","40","3","3","no"]],"title":"Greedy versus optimal (program check)","stepper":false,"noteLast":false}},"tag":"DERIVED","options":["`coins = [1, 2, 8, 9, 5, 10]` and $x = 13$","`coins = [10, 1, 2, 5]` and $x = 25$","`coins = [6, 1, 5, 7]` and $x = 15$","`coins = [1, 6, 7, 10, 15]` and $x = 35$","`coins = [1, 2, 5, 10, 25]` and $x = 40$"],"key":[0,3]}],"practice":[],"remember":{"facts":["Greedy = locally best, never undone; needs a proof.","Huffman: merge two smallest, depth = code length, total = $\\sum f\\ell$.","Activities: earliest finish, start $\\ge$ last finish.","Rooms: peak simultaneous meetings.","Lateness: earliest deadline first.","Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall are DP."],"triggers":[["\"total bits ... Huffman\"","$\\sum f_i\\ell_i$"],["\"message ABCDEF\" (each once)","sum of lengths"],["\"maximum number of activities\"","sort by finish"],["\"minimum rooms\"","peak overlap"],["\"minimum lateness\"","sort by deadline"],["\"counter-example\"","compare greedy with optimal"]],"traps":["Merging the two largest.","Sorting activities by start.","Using > instead of >=.","Believing greedy is always optimal.","Dividing the saving by the new size."],"hooks":["Small-Small-Sum.","Finish Fast, Fit Many.","Due Date First."],"moves":[["Huffman PYQ","list weights, merge two smallest, read depths","3 min","sum of merges = weighted total"],["activity selection","sort by finish, scan with >=","2 min","chosen ones do not overlap"],["meeting rooms","events +1/-1, ends first, peak","2 min","peak at most the number of meetings"],["lateness","sort by deadline, running sum","2 min","brute force on 3 jobs"],["is greedy correct","try a tiny counter-example","2 min","compare with DP"]]},"formulas":[{"name":"Total Huffman bits","tex":"\\sum f_i\\,\\ell_i","cond":"frequencies $f_i$, code lengths $\\ell_i$"},{"name":"Percent saved","tex":"\\frac{old-new}{old}\\times100","cond":"relative to the original"},{"name":"Lateness","tex":"L_i=\\max(0,F_i-D_i)","cond":"$F_i$ = running sum of durations"},{"name":"Merges in Huffman","tex":"n-1","cond":"$n$ symbols"}],"flash":[{"id":"gr-f1","kind":"concept","front":"Huffman: which two trees are merged?","back":"The two with the smallest weights"},{"id":"gr-f2","kind":"formula","front":"Total bits with frequencies","back":"$\\sum f_i\\ell_i$"},{"id":"gr-f3","kind":"concept","front":"Max activities rule","back":"Earliest finish time first"},{"id":"gr-f4","kind":"concept","front":"Minimum rooms equals","back":"Peak number of simultaneous meetings"},{"id":"gr-f5","kind":"concept","front":"Min max lateness rule","back":"Earliest deadline first"},{"id":"gr-f6","kind":"trap","front":"Is Bellman-Ford greedy?","back":"No: dynamic programming"},{"id":"gr-f7","kind":"trap","front":"Is the Huffman tree complete?","back":"No: strict binary, not complete"},{"id":"gr-f8","kind":"concept","front":"Huffman time with a min-heap","back":"$O(n\\log n)$"},{"id":"gr-f9","kind":"concept","front":"Exchange argument","back":"Swap an optimal solution's choice for the greedy one without making it worse"},{"id":"gr-f10","kind":"formula","front":"Huffman total bits shortcut","back":"Sum of all merge weights"},{"id":"gr-f11","kind":"concept","front":"Why rooms = peak overlap","back":"Peak is a lower bound, and the room-filling procedure meets it"},{"id":"gr-f12","kind":"trap","front":"Does idle time help lateness?","back":"Never"}],"test":[{"id":"gr-t1","concept":"Rule","type":"MCQ","stem":"Maximum activities in one room: sort by","options":["start","finish","duration","random"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"Earliest finish.","why":{"A":"","B":"Correct.","C":"","D":""}},{"id":"gr-t2","concept":"Huffman","type":"SA","stem":"Frequencies 1, 1, 2, 4: total bits?","num":{"ans":14,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Merges 2, 4, 8: sum 14."},{"id":"gr-t3","concept":"Rooms","type":"SA","stem":"Meetings (0,3), (1,4), (2,5), (6,7): rooms?","num":{"ans":3,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Three overlap at time 2.5."},{"id":"gr-t4","concept":"Prefix","type":"MCQ","stem":"Which is prefix-free?","options":["0, 01","1, 10","0, 10, 11","1, 11"],"key":[2],"diff":"easy","explain":"No code starts another.","why":{"A":"","B":"","C":"Correct.","D":""}},{"id":"gr-t5","concept":"Lateness","type":"SA","stem":"Two jobs with (duration, deadline) = (2, 2) and (1, 2). What is the minimum possible maximum lateness?","num":{"ans":1,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Both deadlines are 2, so either order is EDF. The second job always finishes at time 3, one unit late. Minimum = 1."},{"id":"gr-t6","concept":"Greedy","type":"MCQ","stem":"Greedy is always optimal:","options":["True","False"],"key":[1],"diff":"easy","explain":"Counter-example: coins {1,3,4}.","why":{"A":"","B":"Correct."}},{"id":"gr-t7","concept":"Huffman","type":"SA","stem":"Frequencies 5, 9, 12, 13, 16, 45: total bits?","num":{"ans":224,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Sum of merges."},{"id":"gr-t8","concept":"Counter","type":"SA","stem":"Coins {1,5,6,9}, x = 11: optimal number of coins?","num":{"ans":2,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"6 + 5."},{"id":"gr-t9","concept":"Rooms","type":"SA","stem":"Meetings (0,4), (1,3), (2,6), (5,8), (7,9), (3,5): rooms?","num":{"ans":3,"tol":0},"diff":"easy","explain":"Peak overlap 3."}],"checklist":["I can build a Huffman tree and read lengths.","I can tell total bits from sum of lengths.","I can do activity selection with >=.","I can count rooms by sweeping.","I can compute lateness with EDF.","I can find a coin counter-example."],"cheat":[{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"Huffman: merge two smallest; total $=\\sum f\\ell$ = sum of merge weights; strict binary tree; prefix codes."},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"Activities: sort by finish, take if start >= last finish. Rooms: peak overlap. Lateness: sort by deadline, running sum."},{"t":"co","kind":"formula","md":"Greedy needs a proof. Coins {1,3,4} for 6 breaks it. Bellman-Ford and Floyd-Warshall are DP."}]};
